    (mid, $S$Operations & Workflows — Full Overview$S$, $S$operations-workflows-full-overview$S$, $C$<h2><strong>Purpose</strong></h2><p>Give your team a shared way to <strong>find real operational problems</strong>, design <strong>clear, repeatable workflows</strong>, and <strong>improve them with data</strong>—adding automation and agentic support only where it’s stable and safe.</p><h2><strong>Scope</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>In:</strong> Problem discovery, scoping, mapping (L0–L3), roles/ownership, acceptance criteria, baseline metrics (CT/WT/FPY/WIP), software stack discipline, automation/agent standards, cadences for review.</li><li><strong>Out (covered later):</strong> Vendor/tool deep dives, industry-specific playbooks, heavy AI buildcraft.</li></ul><h2><strong>Outcomes</strong></h2><p>After this classroom, a founder/manager can:</p><ol><li>Frame messy situations as <strong>precise problems</strong> with measurable success criteria.</li><li>Map any process from <strong>Trigger → Done</strong> with swimlanes, handoffs, and decisions.</li><li>Assign <strong>single-point accountability</strong> (one owner per outcome) and clearly defined roles.</li><li>Embed <strong>acceptance criteria &amp; quality gates</strong> to prevent defects from moving downstream.</li><li>Baseline and use <strong>CT, WT, FPY, WIP</strong> (plus lead time/throughput) to find the bottleneck.</li><li><strong>Score automation opportunities</strong>, rationalize the software stack, and add <strong>agentic workflows</strong> with humans in the loop.</li><li>Run a <strong>weekly ops huddle</strong> and <strong>monthly improvement cycle</strong> that actually sticks.</li></ol><h2><strong>Operating Principles</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>As-is before to-be.</strong> Improve reality, not wish lists.</li><li><strong>One owner per outcome.</strong> Accountability isn’t a committee.</li><li><strong>Trigger &amp; Done are explicit.</strong> No start signal, no finish line = churn and late revenue.</li><li><strong>Complete-input rule.</strong> Work doesn’t start without required inputs.</li><li><strong>Small batch, low WIP.</strong> Predictability beats heroics.</li><li><strong>Version &amp; review.</strong> Every map/SOP shows owner, version, date, next review.</li><li><strong>Automate the stable.</strong> Humans own outcomes; automations/agents own tightly scoped tasks.</li></ul><h2><strong>Core Vocabulary</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Trigger:</strong> The unambiguous event that starts the process (e.g., “signed agreement recorded”).</li><li><strong>Done:</strong> Business-meaningful finish (e.g., “first value delivered &amp; billing active”).</li><li><strong>Stage (L1) / Procedure (L2) / Task (L3):</strong> Increasing detail from major phases → steps → clicks.</li><li><strong>Swimlanes:</strong> Lanes by role/team to show ownership and handoffs.</li><li><strong>RACIQ:</strong> Responsible (does) • Accountable (owns outcome) • Consulted • Informed • Quality gate approver.</li></ul><h2><strong>Core Flow Metrics (how you’ll manage)</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Cycle Time (CT):</strong> Active work time per stage or end-to-end.<ul><li>Measure: finished_at − started_at (pause when no hands are on the work). Use <strong>median</strong>.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Wait Time (WT):</strong> Idle time between stages.<ul><li>Measure: started_at(next_stage) − finished_at(this_stage).</li></ul></li><li><strong>First-Pass Yield (FPY):</strong> % that pass a stage <strong>without rework</strong>.<ul><li>Measure per stage: no-rework units ÷ units entered.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Work-in-Process (WIP):</strong> Items started but not yet Done.<ul><li>Control: Set WIP caps at bottleneck queues to stabilize lead time.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Also track:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Lead Time:</strong> Trigger → Done.</li><li><strong>Throughput:</strong> Units completed per period.</li><li><strong>Aging WIP:</strong> Oldest item in each stage.</li><li><strong>Simple law:</strong> <strong>Little’s Law</strong> → WIP = Throughput × Lead Time.</li></ul><h2><strong>Problem Mapping (finding the real issue)</strong></h2><ol><li><strong>Define the problem:</strong> Observation → Impact (revenue/cost/risk/CSAT) → Scope boundaries.</li><li><strong>Hypotheses:</strong> What might be causing it (label assumptions).</li><li><strong>Success criteria:</strong> Concrete targets (e.g., lead time ≤ 7 biz days, FPY ≥ 90%).</li><li><strong>SIPOC (15 min):</strong> Top Suppliers, Inputs, Process (one sentence), Outputs, Customers. Locks scope fast.</li><li><strong>Gemba:</strong> Watch 3–5 real cases. Capture true steps, tools, timestamps, decisions, rework loops, exceptions.</li></ol><h2><strong>Workflow Design (how you’ll document reality)</strong></h2><h3><strong>Levels of Detail</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>L0 Value Stream:</strong> End-to-end from request to delivery/cash.</li><li><strong>L1 Stages (6–12 boxes):</strong> Major phases and handoffs.</li><li><strong>L2 Procedures:</strong> Step sequences per stage (Owner • Inputs • Tool • Actions • Output • “Done”).</li><li><strong>L3 Tasks:</strong> Click-by-click with screenshots—<strong>only</strong> where risk/variability is high.</li></ul><h3><strong>Visual Methods</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Basic flowchart (default):</strong> Start/End, Process, Decision (with explicit yes/no criteria), Data, Connectors.</li><li><strong>Swimlane flowchart:</strong> For ownership/handoffs across roles.</li><li><strong>Value stream with data boxes:</strong> Under each stage show <strong>CT, WT, FPY, WIP</strong> (medians/rates).</li><li><strong>BPMN-lite (optional):</strong> Use sparingly for timers/events when complexity demands it.</li></ul><h3><strong>Minimum Map Standards</strong></h3><ul><li>Header with <strong>Process, Owner, Version, Date, Next Review</strong>.</li><li><strong>Trigger &amp; Done</strong> at the top.</li><li><strong>Swimlanes</strong> and <strong>RACIQ</strong> per stage.</li><li><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong> at key gates (3–6 bullets).</li><li><strong>Metrics boxes</strong> per stage: CT, WT, FPY, WIP (from last 20–30 items).</li><li><strong>Naming/storage:</strong> Strict conventions (YYYYMMDD_Process_Step_v#) and one shared location.</li><li><strong>Data you capture per unit × stage:</strong> entered_at • started_at • finished_at • first_pass (y/n) • rework_count • owner.</li></ul><h2><strong>Software Stack Discipline (so data doesn’t fight you)</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>System of Record per object</strong> (Customer, Order, Project, Invoice, Candidate, Asset). Integrate <em>to</em> the SoR; no duplicate entry.</li><li><strong>Integration map:</strong> Show systems, fields exchanged, triggers, and owners from Trigger → Done.</li><li><strong>Data hygiene:</strong> Required fields, dropdowns over free text, canonical IDs, validation pre-checks.</li><li><strong>Tool governance:</strong> Add a tool only if it removes a bottleneck or reduces risk/cost. Quarterly usage/cost/overlap audit.</li></ul><h2><strong>Automation Opportunities (how to remove redundancy)</strong></h2><p><strong>Good candidates:</strong> High-frequency + low-variability steps (routing, notifications, renames, reconciliations, pre-fill, status sync).<br /> <strong>Score each (1–5):</strong> Volume • Minutes/run • Error impact • Rule clarity • Data quality. Prioritize top totals.<br /> <strong>Readiness gates (must pass before automating):</strong></p><ol><li>L1/L2 map versioned • 2) Owner + acceptance criteria set • 3) Inputs standardized • 4) Baseline metrics captured • 5) Exception/rollback policy • 6) Pilot 10–20 cases, ≥95% success.</li></ol><h2><strong>Agentic Workflows (safe, scoped, observable)</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>When to use:</strong> After stabilization, for triage, enrichment, routing, summarization, reconciliation, templated drafting, monitoring.</li><li><strong>Guardrails:</strong> Scope boundary • allowed tools/functions • least-privilege access • cost/rate limits • audit logs.</li><li><strong>Human-in-the-loop:</strong> Auto-escalate on low confidence or exceptions; humans own final outcomes.</li><li><strong>Metrics:</strong> Success rate • intervention rate • cost/run • MTTR on escalations • drift alerts.</li></ul><h2><strong>Cadences &amp; Governance</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Weekly Ops Huddle (15–20 min):</strong> Lead Time, FPY, SLA hit %, Aging WIP; oldest item per stage; one constraint to attack this month.</li><li><strong>Monthly Improvement Cycle:</strong> Fix the current bottleneck (policy, capacity, WIP, data quality, automation).</li><li><strong>Quarterly Review:</strong> Re-baseline metrics, retire dead steps, update SLAs, audit stack, refresh training.</li><li><strong>Change control:</strong> Impact assess → pilot → release → rollback plan; update version headers everywhere.</li></ul><h2><strong>Rollout Sequence (how we’ll execute)</strong></h2><ol><li>Pick one high-impact process; define <strong>Trigger → Done</strong> and the <strong>Accountable owner</strong>.</li><li>SIPOC to lock scope; set success criteria.</li><li>Gemba on 3–5 cases; draft <strong>L1 with swimlanes</strong> and decision points.</li><li>Add <strong>acceptance criteria</strong> and quality gates where risk justifies.</li><li>Baseline <strong>CT/WT/FPY/WIP</strong> per stage; identify the <strong>constraint</strong>.</li><li>Design a focused <strong>to-be</strong> improvement; pilot on 10–20 items; compare to baseline.</li><li>Version, train, set <strong>WIP caps</strong>, launch the <strong>weekly huddle</strong>.</li><li>Add <strong>automation/agent support</strong> to stable, deterministic steps with guardrails.</li></ol><p>If you’re good with this, tell me <strong>which process you want to map first inside this classroom</strong> (e.g., client onboarding, order-to-cash, request-to-resolution). I’ll run the exact build sequence above, step by step.</p><h5><strong>You said:</strong></h5><p>let's put the lessons in the most logical order based on the users need to learn the information. </p><h6><strong>ChatGPT said:</strong></h6><p>Thought for 1m 52s</p><p>Perfect. Here’s the <strong>most logical learning sequence</strong> for the <strong>Operations &amp; Workflows</strong> classroom—clean, industry-agnostic, and skill-building in the right order.</p><h1><strong>Lesson Order (from zero → scale)</strong></h1><ol><li><strong>Foundations &amp; Core Concepts</strong><br /> Trigger → Done, L0–L3 levels, swimlanes, RACIQ, CT/WT/FPY/WIP, operating principles.</li><li><strong>Problem Mapping &amp; SIPOC</strong><br /> Define the problem, impact, scope, hypotheses, success criteria; lock scope fast with SIPOC.</li><li><strong>Gemba: Observe &amp; Capture Data</strong><br /> Watch 3–5 real cases; record steps, timestamps, tools, decisions, exceptions.</li><li><strong>L0 Value Stream (End-to-End)</strong><br /> Map request → delivery/cash; set boundaries and the meaningful finish line.</li><li><strong>L1 Stages + Swimlanes (Ownership)</strong><br /> 6–12 stages, explicit handoffs, one accountable owner per stage; decision points with criteria.</li><li><strong>L2 Procedures (Quality by Design)</strong><br /> For each stage: Owner • Inputs • Tool • Actions • Output • “Done.” Add acceptance criteria and complete-input rules.</li><li><strong>L3 Task Steps (As Needed)</strong><br /> Click-by-click only where risk/variability is high; screenshots/field rules.</li><li><strong>Instrumentation: CT, WT, FPY, WIP</strong><br /> Set the data schema, baseline from last 20–30 items, and add per-stage metric boxes.</li><li><strong>Flow Control: Bottlenecks, WIP Limits &amp; SLAs</strong><br /> Identify the constraint; cap WIP at queues; define realistic SLAs tied to capacity.</li><li><strong>Ops Dashboard &amp; Weekly Huddles</strong><br /> Lead time, throughput, FPY, aging WIP; review oldest item per stage; pick one constraint per month.</li><li><strong>Software Stack Discipline &amp; Integration Mapping</strong><br /> System of Record per object, data hygiene standards, one integration map from Trigger → Done.</li><li><strong>Automation Opportunity Scoring &amp; Readiness Gates</strong><br /> Score high-volume/low-variability steps; verify stability; pilot with guardrails before scaling.</li><li><strong>Agentic Workflow Patterns (Human-in-the-Loop)</strong><br /> Safe scopes, triggers, permissions, observability, escalation on low confidence.</li><li><strong>Incident Response &amp; Post-Mortems</strong><br /> Severity levels, comms, blameless analysis, fixes, verification.</li><li><strong>Change Management &amp; Version Control</strong><br /> Impact assess → pilot → release → rollback; version headers and a single source of truth.</li><li><strong>Continuous Improvement Cadence (Monthly/Quarterly)</strong><br /> Re-baseline, retire dead steps, refresh SLAs/training, audit the stack.</li></ol><p><strong>Why this order works:</strong> you start by framing the problem and observing reality, then map and assign ownership, embed quality, measure the flow, control it, and only then layer tools, automation, and agents—finished with governance and a cadence that keeps gains from slipping.</p>$C$, 1, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 1 — Foundations & Core Concepts$S$, $S$lesson-1-foundations-core-concepts$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>Operations is how work travels from <strong>Trigger → Done → Cash</strong>. This lesson installs the shared language and guardrails you’ll use across every workflow:</p><ul><li>clear <strong>start/finish conditions</strong></li><li>the right <strong>level of detail</strong> (L0–L3)</li><li><strong>ownership</strong> by role (swimlanes, RACIQ)</li><li>crisp <strong>acceptance criteria</strong> at handoffs</li><li>core <strong>flow metrics</strong> (CT, WT, FPY, WIP)</li><li>disciplined <strong>versioning, inputs, and WIP limits</strong></li></ul><p>You’ll document the <strong>as-is</strong> process first, measure it, then improve. Automations and agents come <strong>after</strong> stability.</p><h3><strong>Importance</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Predictable delivery = predictable revenue.</strong> Stable lead times let you promise confidently and invoice sooner.</li><li><strong>Lower cost per unit.</strong> Less rework and waiting; fewer people touching the same job.</li><li><strong>Consistent quality.</strong> Acceptance criteria and gates stop defects from moving downstream.</li><li><strong>Founder freedom.</strong> You manage by dashboard and cadence—not memory and heroics.</li><li><strong>Automation-ready.</strong> Clean maps + clear rules = safe candidates for bots/agents later.</li></ul><h3><strong>Core Concepts</strong></h3><p><strong>1) Trigger &amp; Done</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Trigger:</strong> the unambiguous signal work starts (e.g., “signed agreement recorded in system”).</li><li><strong>Done:</strong> the business finish line (e.g., “first value delivered and billing active”).<br /> No Trigger/Done = endless loops and late cash.</li></ul><p><strong>2) Levels of Detail (L0 → L3)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>L0 Value Stream:</strong> request → delivery/cash.</li><li><strong>L1 Stages (6–12 boxes):</strong> major phases and handoffs.</li><li><strong>L2 Procedures:</strong> for each stage, define <strong>Owner • Inputs • Tool • Actions • Output • “Done.”</strong></li><li><strong>L3 Task Steps:</strong> click-by-click with screenshots only where risk/variability is high.</li></ul><p><strong>3) Swimlanes &amp; Ownership (RACIQ)</strong></p><ul><li>Lanes = <strong>roles/teams</strong>; each step lives in the lane of the <strong>Responsible</strong> doer.</li><li>One <strong>Accountable</strong> owner per stage (not a committee).</li><li><strong>C</strong>onsulted, <strong>I</strong>nformed as needed; add <strong>Q</strong>uality gate approver at risky gates.</li></ul><p><strong>4) Acceptance Criteria &amp; Gates</strong><br /> Short, objective checklists (3–6 bullets) required to advance.<br /> Example (generic handoff):</p><ul><li>Required fields present and valid</li><li>Attachments named to standard (YYYYMMDD_Object_DocType_v#)</li><li>No open blockers noted</li></ul><p><strong>5) Core Flow Metrics</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cycle Time (CT):</strong> active work time per stage. CT = finished_at − started_at (pause when idle).</li><li><strong>Wait Time (WT):</strong> idle time between stages. WT = start_next − finish_this.</li><li><strong>First-Pass Yield (FPY):</strong> % passing a stage with <strong>no</strong> rework.</li><li><strong>Work-in-Process (WIP):</strong> items started but not Done.<br /> Also track <strong>Lead Time</strong> (Trigger → Done) and <strong>Throughput</strong> (units per period).<br /> Remember <strong>Little’s Law:</strong> WIP = Throughput × Lead Time.</li></ul><p><strong>6) Complete-Input Rule</strong><br /> Work <strong>does not start</strong> unless required inputs are present. This alone crushes rework.</p><p><strong>7) WIP Limits &amp; Small Batches</strong><br /> Cap open items at the bottleneck queue to stabilize lead time. More WIP ≠ more speed.</p><p><strong>8) Versioning &amp; Single Source of Truth</strong><br /> Every map/SOP shows <strong>Owner, Version, Date, Next Review</strong>. Store once; link everywhere.</p><h3><strong>Common Mistakes</strong></h3><ul><li>Mapping the ideal instead of the real (skip the <em>to-be</em> until the <em>as-is</em> is stable).</li><li>Vague starts/finishes; “kinda done” work stacks up and cash lags.</li><li>Role fog—no single accountable owner per stage.</li><li>Starting work with incomplete inputs.</li><li>Tool sprawl; no declared <strong>system of record</strong> per object.</li><li>Over-approvals that don’t add quality.</li><li>Measuring totals/averages instead of <strong>stage-level CT/WT/FPY/WIP</strong>.</li><li>Raising WIP to “go faster” (it slows you down).</li></ul><h3><strong>Implementation Steps (do these now)</strong></h3><p><strong>Phase A — Frame &amp; Observe (60–90 min)</strong></p><ol><li>Pick one high-impact process. Write its <strong>Trigger</strong> and <strong>Done</strong> in a single line each.</li><li>Name the <strong>Accountable</strong> owner for the end-to-end outcome.</li><li>Run a quick <strong>SIPOC</strong> (top 3–5 Suppliers/Inputs/Outputs/Customers).</li><li><strong>Gemba:</strong> watch 3–5 real cases; capture true steps, timestamps, tools, decisions, and exceptions.</li></ol><p><strong>Phase B — Map &amp; Assign (60 min)</strong><br /> 5) Draft <strong>L1</strong> with <strong>6–12 stages</strong> in <strong>swimlanes</strong> by role.<br /> 6) Add <strong>decision diamonds</strong> with explicit yes/no criteria.<br /> 7) For each stage, draft <strong>L2</strong> fields: <strong>Owner, Inputs, Tool, Actions, Output, “Done.”</strong><br /> 8) Add <strong>acceptance criteria</strong> at risky handoffs (3–6 bullets).</p><p><strong>Phase C — Instrument &amp; Stabilize (60 min)</strong><br /> 9) From the last 20–30 items, baseline <strong>CT, WT, FPY, WIP</strong> per stage (use medians).<br /> 10) Identify the <strong>constraint</strong> (longest effective cycle or largest queue).<br /> 11) Set initial <strong>WIP limits</strong> at the constraint; enforce the <strong>Complete-Input Rule</strong>.<br /> 12) Add a header to the map (<strong>Owner, Version, Date, Next Review</strong>) and store it in the shared location.</p><p><strong>Phase D — Manage the Flow (ongoing)</strong><br /> 13) Stand up a <strong>weekly ops huddle (15–20 min):</strong> lead time, FPY, aging WIP; oldest item per stage; one constraint to fix this month.<br /> 14) Only after two stable weeks, list <strong>candidate automations/agents</strong> (don’t build yet).</p><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>Put <strong>Trigger</strong> and <strong>Done</strong> at the very top of every map—non-negotiable.</li><li>Keep <strong>L1 to 6–12 boxes</strong>. If you need more, you’re at L2.</li><li>Use <strong>medians</strong> for CT/WT; means get distorted by outliers.</li><li>If <strong>WT ≫ CT</strong> at a handoff, that’s your first target—fix inputs, ownership, or WIP.</li><li>Start each stage with a <strong>3–6 bullet acceptance checklist</strong>; it slashes rework.</li><li>Cap WIP at the bottleneck queue before adding headcount.</li><li>Don’t automate for two stable weeks; otherwise you’ll <strong>scale defects</strong>.</li><li>Stamp every map/SOP with <strong>Owner, Version, Date, Next Review</strong> to kill “which doc is latest?” drama.</li></ul>$C$, 2, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 2 — Problem Mapping & SIPOC$S$, $S$lesson-2-problem-mapping-sipoc$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>Before you “optimize,” you need the <strong>real</strong> problem, tight scope, and a shared picture of how work flows. This lesson installs a fast, repeatable way to turn noise into a <strong>precise problem</strong>, anchor scope with <strong>SIPOC</strong>, and prep clean inputs for your first L1 map.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li>Stops you from solving symptoms (rework, delays) instead of causes (unclear inputs, role fog, bad handoffs).</li><li>Creates alignment across teams in under an hour.</li><li>Produces the <strong>minimum data</strong> you need to measure CT, WT, FPY, and WIP in Lesson 3/4.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end, you’ll have:</p><ol><li>A one-line <strong>Problem Statement</strong> + 3–5 <strong>Success Criteria</strong>.</li><li>A 15-minute <strong>SIPOC</strong> that locks boundaries and players.</li><li>A short <strong>hypotheses list</strong> (labeled as assumptions) to test during Gemba.</li><li>A trimmed <strong>evidence pack</strong>: last 20–30 instances of the workflow with timestamps/owners.</li></ol><h3><strong>Step-by-step</strong></h3><h4><strong>1) Write the Problem Statement (10 minutes)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Format:</strong> “We’re experiencing <strong>[observable issue]</strong>, causing <strong>[impact metric(s)]</strong>, most often in <strong>[scope/boundary]</strong>.”</li><li><strong>Examples:</strong><ul><li>“Customer requests linger unassigned &gt;48h, delaying first delivery and pushing cash collection beyond 30 days.”</li></ul></li><li><strong>Success Criteria (3–5):</strong> Concrete, measurable (e.g., “First response ≤ 4h; Lead Time ≤ 7 biz days; FPY ≥ 90%”).</li></ul><h4><strong>2) Surface Hypotheses (5–10 minutes)</strong></h4><p>List what you <strong>think</strong> is causing it (mark as <em>assumption</em>): incomplete inputs, unclear owner, tool handoff, approvals, capacity, etc. You’ll verify/kill these in Gemba.</p><h4><strong>3) SIPOC to lock scope (15 minutes)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Suppliers:</strong> Who provides inputs (internal/external).</li><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> The few critical things required to start (forms, files, approvals).</li><li><strong>Process (one sentence):</strong> “Converts [inputs] into [outputs] under [constraints].”</li><li><strong>Outputs:</strong> What leaves the process (deliverables, confirmations, invoices).</li><li><strong>Customers:</strong> Who consumes the outputs (internal team, end customer, finance).<br /> Timebox it. If it takes &gt;20 minutes, your scope is too big.</li></ul><h4><strong>4) Build the Evidence Pack (20–30 minutes)</strong></h4><p>Pull <strong>the last 20–30 items</strong> that ran through this flow. For each, capture:<br /> unit_id • stage_name • entered_at • started_at • finished_at • owner • first_pass (y/n) • rework_count<br /> This small dataset lets you baseline <strong>CT/WT/FPY/WIP</strong> next.</p><h4><strong>5) Prep for Mapping (10 minutes)</strong></h4><ul><li>Identify <strong>Accountable owner</strong> (one person) for the end-to-end outcome.</li><li>List <strong>roles</strong> involved (these will become <strong>swimlanes</strong>).</li><li>Draft initial <strong>Trigger</strong> (“work starts when…”) and <strong>Done</strong> (“business-meaningful finish is…”).</li></ul><h3><strong>Quality checks (pass before moving on)</strong></h3><ul><li>Problem statement references an <strong>observable</strong> issue and a <strong>business impact</strong>.</li><li>Success criteria are <strong>numeric</strong> (time, yield, volume).</li><li>SIPOC completed with <strong>top 3–5</strong> entries each—no novels.</li><li>Evidence pack has <strong>20–30 recent items</strong> with timestamps and owners.</li><li>A single <strong>Accountable</strong> owner is named; Trigger and Done are explicit.</li></ul><h3><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Symptom masquerading as problem:</strong> “We need automation.” → Reframe to the operational issue (e.g., “Requests idle 2.3 days before assignment”).</li><li><strong>Vague scope:</strong> “Onboarding takes too long.” → “Closed-Won to Kickoff exceeds 10 biz days for Tier B accounts.”</li><li><strong>Skipping data:</strong> Opinions battle; nobody wins. → Pull the last 20–30 items and measure.</li><li><strong>Too many inputs:</strong> Start with the <strong>minimum</strong> required to begin work; the rest can follow.</li><li><strong>Committee ownership:</strong> Name one accountable human.</li></ul><h3><strong>Suggested tools (optional)</strong></h3><p>Use any stack you already own. If you need a workspace quickly:</p><ul><li>Mapping/whiteboard: Miro, Lucidchart, Whimsical, diagrams.net</li><li>Data capture: Google Sheets, Airtable</li><li>Docs/notes: Notion, Google Docs</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>One sentence only</strong> for the Process in SIPOC—forces clarity.</li><li>Put <strong>Trigger/Done</strong> at the top of every doc from day one; it kills ambiguity.</li><li>If you can’t gather 20–30 recent items, your process isn’t occurring enough—<strong>pick a higher-volume target</strong> first.</li><li>Keep a <strong>hypotheses kill list</strong> during Gemba; deleting bad assumptions is progress.</li><li>Don’t add tools to “solve” the problem yet. <strong>Stabilize the map first</strong>—automation comes after.</li><li>For alignment, run a 20-minute read-out: Problem → Success Criteria → SIPOC → Trigger/Done → Next steps. Keep it tight.</li></ul><div class="lesson-template"><h3>📄 Template for this lesson</h3><p>Download and use the worksheet that goes with this lesson:</p><a class="dl" href="/templates/operations-workflow/problem-sipoc-one-pager.docx" download="Problem + SIPOC One‑Pager.docx">⬇ Download Problem + SIPOC One-Pager</a></div>$C$, 3, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 3 — Gemba: Observe & Capture Data$S$, $S$lesson-3-gemba-observe-capture-data$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>You can’t fix what you haven’t seen. <strong>Gemba</strong> means “go to the place where the work happens.” This lesson shows you how to <strong>watch real work</strong>, capture <strong>clean timestamps and facts</strong>, and build the small dataset you’ll use to calculate <strong>CT, WT, FPY, and WIP</strong>—without debating opinions.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li>Replaces “I think” with <strong>measured reality</strong>.</li><li>Exposes hidden waits, rework loops, and unclear ownership that maps alone won’t reveal.</li><li>Produces the <strong>minimum viable dataset</strong> we’ll use to pick the true bottleneck in the next lessons.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end, you’ll have:</p><ol><li>A <strong>sampling plan</strong> (which items to observe and when).</li><li>A simple <strong>event log</strong> with timestamps for 20–30 recent items.</li><li>Clear tags for <strong>first-pass vs. rework</strong> and <strong>reason codes</strong> for defects/waits.</li><li>Consent and privacy boxes checked for any recordings.</li></ol><h3><strong>Step-by-step</strong></h3><h4><strong>1) Build a sampling plan (10 minutes)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Volume:</strong> Aim for <strong>20–30 recent items</strong> of the same process.</li><li><strong>Coverage:</strong> Include at least <strong>2 different days</strong> and <strong>2 different staff</strong> if possible.</li><li><strong>Timing:</strong> Observe across <strong>handoffs</strong> (where stages change lanes).</li><li><strong>Mix:</strong> Include <strong>one exception case</strong> (edge case) if it’s &gt;10% of volume.</li></ul><h4><strong>2) Collect consent (5 minutes)</strong></h4><ul><li>If you record screens or calls, get <strong>explicit consent</strong> and state purpose, scope, and retention.</li><li>Use your existing policy or write a 2-sentence notice. Screen recorders: Loom, Zoom, OBS.</li></ul><h4><strong>3) Watch the work (30–90 minutes total)</strong></h4><ul><li>Sit with (or screen-share) the doer. Ask them to <strong>work as usual</strong>.</li><li>Capture <strong>what triggers start</strong>, <strong>what “done” means</strong>, tools touched, exact <strong>decision rules</strong>, and every <strong>handoff</strong>.</li><li>Time each stage: note <strong>entered_at</strong> (arrived in queue), <strong>started_at</strong> (hands on), <strong>finished_at</strong> (hands off).</li><li>Log any bounce-backs to earlier steps (that’s <strong>rework</strong>).</li></ul><h4><strong>4) Maintain a simple event log (live or same day)</strong></h4><p><strong>Minimum fields per unit × stage:</strong></p><ul><li>unit_id</li><li>stage_name</li><li>entered_at (arrives in queue)</li><li>started_at (work begins)</li><li>finished_at (work ends)</li><li>owner (Responsible role)</li><li>first_pass (yes/no)</li><li>rework_count (0,1,2,…)</li><li>wait_reason_code (if WT is high)</li><li>notes (brief, factual)</li></ul><p>You can do this in Google Sheets or Airtable. Use Toggl Track or Clockify if you want lightweight timers.</p><h4><strong>5) Tag first-pass vs. rework precisely</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>First-pass (yes):</strong> The unit <strong>advances to the next stage</strong> with <strong>no corrections</strong>.</li><li><strong>Rework (no):</strong> Any bounce-back, edit, or repeat of the same stage. Increment rework_count.</li><li>If a stage is revisited, create a <strong>new row</strong> for that same unit_id + stage_name and increase rework_count.</li></ul><h4><strong>6) Use a small, consistent reason-code set</strong></h4><p>When waits or rework occur, pick <strong>one</strong> reason code (don’t stack):</p><ul><li><strong>MI</strong> – Missing/Incomplete Input</li><li><strong>AL</strong> – Approval Lag</li><li><strong>AD</strong> – Access Delay / Permissions</li><li><strong>TD</strong> – Tool Down / Integration Fail</li><li><strong>DQ</strong> – Data Quality / Format Error</li><li><strong>CP</strong> – Capacity / Over WIP</li><li><strong>SC</strong> – Scope Change</li><li><strong>CD</strong> – Customer Delay<br /> Keep this list short so tagging stays accurate.</li></ul><h4><strong>7) Handle parallel or batched work</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Parallel steps:</strong> If two stages run concurrently, record each stage normally; end-to-end CT uses the <strong>longer branch</strong> for that segment.</li><li><strong>Batching:</strong> If work is processed in batches (e.g., approvals at 4pm), record <strong>entered_at</strong> when the item joins the batch and <strong>started_at</strong> when that batch begins.</li></ul><h4><strong>8) Sanity-check the data (15 minutes)</strong></h4><ul><li>For each row, verify finished_at ≥ started_at ≥ entered_at.</li><li>Spot <strong>negative or missing</strong> times; fix immediately.</li><li>Sample <strong>5 items</strong> and walk them end-to-end to ensure the sequence is complete.</li></ul><h3><strong>How we’ll compute the metrics (from your log)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>CT (per stage):</strong> finished_at − started_at (pause clock if work stops).</li><li><strong>WT (A→B):</strong> started_at(B) − finished_at(A) for the same unit_id.</li><li><strong>FPY (per stage):</strong> count(first_pass==yes) / count(entered) over your 20–30 items.</li><li><strong>WIP (snapshot):</strong> count of units <strong>started but not Done</strong> at a given time; or use the current open count by stage from your board.</li></ul><p>Use <strong>medians</strong> for CT/WT. Stage-level FPY shows where defects originate. We’ll assemble the <strong>per-stage data boxes</strong> next lesson.</p><h3><strong>Quality checks (pass before moving on)</strong></h3><ul><li>You’ve observed <strong>3–5 real cases</strong> end-to-end (not just heard about them).</li><li>You have <strong>20–30 rows of recent items</strong> with valid entered_at / started_at / finished_at.</li><li><strong>first_pass</strong> and <strong>rework_count</strong> are filled in where applicable.</li><li>You used <strong>one</strong> reason code per wait/defect when needed.</li><li>Consent and privacy boxes are checked for any recordings.</li></ul><h3><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Shadow coaching:</strong> People change behavior because you’re watching. → Ask them to <strong>narrate</strong> what they’d normally do and compare to a past item.</li><li><strong>Vague timestamps:</strong> “Sometime in the afternoon.” → Require <strong>clock times</strong> (hh:mm) or use a timer tool.</li><li><strong>Over-tagging reasons:</strong> One event, three codes. → Pick the <strong>primary</strong> reason only.</li><li><strong>Under-counting rework:</strong> Editing a file “quickly” still counts. → Any redo of the same stage = rework.</li><li><strong>Skipping exception paths:</strong> They’re 15% of flow but 80% of pain. → Observe at least one exception case.</li><li><strong>No owner recorded:</strong> You can’t fix a handoff you can’t attribute. → Fill the <strong>owner</strong> field every time.</li></ul><h3><strong>Suggested tools (optional)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Screen recording / calls:</strong> Loom, Zoom, OBS</li><li><strong>Time capture:</strong> Toggl Track, Clockify</li><li><strong>Logging &amp; analysis:</strong> Google Sheets, Airtable</li><li><strong>Issue/defect tracking (optional):</strong> Jira, Linear</li><li><strong>Task boards (for WIP snapshots):</strong> Trello, Asana</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>If you can’t observe live, <strong>reconstruct</strong> from system logs (ticket history, file timestamps, commit history).</li><li>Put your <strong>reason-code list</strong> at the top of the log so tagging stays consistent.</li><li>Start your clock only when <strong>hands touch the work</strong> (that’s CT). Everything before that is <strong>WT</strong>.</li><li>Don’t chase precision you don’t need. <strong>±1 minute</strong> is fine—consistency beats perfection.</li><li>If a handoff causes long waits, add a <strong>Complete-Input checklist</strong> before it in the map.</li><li>Tag one improvement idea <strong>per observation</strong> while it’s fresh, but don’t change the process yet—we measure first, then move.</li></ul><div class="lesson-template"><h3>📄 Template for this lesson</h3><p>Download and use the worksheet that goes with this lesson:</p><a class="dl" href="/templates/operations-workflow/template-2-event-log.xlsx" download="Template_2_Event_Log.xlsx">⬇ Download Event Log (Template 2)</a></div>$C$, 4, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 4 — L0 Value Stream Mapping (End-to-End)$S$, $S$lesson-4-l0-value-stream-mapping-end-to-end$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>L0 mapping shows the <strong>entire journey from Trigger → Done</strong> in a single line of sight. It’s the bird’s-eye view that anchors scope, exposes major waits, and sets the stage for L1/L2 detail.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li>Aligns everyone on <strong>what the process really is</strong> (no side quests).</li><li>Reveals where time actually disappears (handoffs, approvals, rework).</li><li>Establishes the <strong>business finish line</strong> that ties ops to cash and satisfaction.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end, you’ll have:</p><ol><li>A one-page <strong>value stream map</strong> with 6–10 stages from <strong>Trigger → Done</strong>.</li><li>Clear <strong>customers</strong> and <strong>outputs</strong> for the stream.</li><li>Draft <strong>metric boxes</strong> under each stage (CT, WT, FPY, WIP) ready to fill from your Lesson 3 data.</li></ol><h3><strong>Step-by-step</strong></h3><h4><strong>1) Lock the boundaries (5–10 min)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Trigger:</strong> The moment work officially starts (e.g., “request received in system”).</li><li><strong>Done:</strong> The <strong>business</strong> finish line (e.g., “value delivered &amp; billing active”).</li><li>Name the <strong>Accountable Owner</strong> for the end-to-end outcome (one person).</li></ul><h4><strong>2) Name the customers &amp; outputs (5 min)</strong></h4><ul><li>Who consumes the outcome (internal or external)?</li><li>What are the <strong>must-have outputs</strong> (deliverable, confirmation, invoice, record update)?</li></ul><h4><strong>3) Draft the stage spine (15–20 min)</strong></h4><ul><li>Create <strong>6–10 big blocks</strong> that cover the whole journey. Use <strong>verbs</strong>: <em>Capture request → Qualify → Prepare → Execute → Validate → Deliver → Bill → Close</em>.</li><li>Keep it linear for L0. Loops/branches wait until L1.</li></ul><h4><strong>4) Place high-level handoffs (5–10 min)</strong></h4><ul><li>Mark where ownership changes (team/role). These are wait risks.</li><li>Add a note if a stage depends on a <strong>complete-input</strong> checklist.</li></ul><h4><strong>5) Add L0 metric boxes (10–15 min)</strong></h4><p>Under each stage, reserve fields you’ll fill with your Lesson 3 data:</p><ul><li><strong>CT (median)</strong></li><li><strong>WT (median to next stage)</strong></li><li><strong>FPY (%)</strong></li><li><strong>WIP (count)</strong></li></ul><h4><strong>6) Sanity checks (5 min)</strong></h4><ul><li>Every stage advances the customer toward <strong>Done</strong>.</li><li>No stage exists just to “forward” or “check email.” Fold those into a real step.</li><li>Trigger and Done are <strong>visible at the top</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Quality checks (pass before moving on)</strong></h3><ul><li>The map fits on <strong>one page</strong>, is read left → right, and has <strong>6–10 stages</strong>.</li><li><strong>Trigger, Done, Accountable Owner</strong> are clearly stated.</li><li>Each stage has a placeholder for <strong>CT/WT/FPY/WIP</strong>.</li><li>Major <strong>handoffs</strong> are marked (where roles change).</li></ul><h3><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Too much detail at L0.</strong> → If you need more than ~10 blocks, you’re at L1.</li><li><strong>Vague finish line.</strong> → Redefine <strong>Done</strong> in business terms (value &amp; cash), not internal admin.</li><li><strong>Approval-heavy flows.</strong> → Keep only approvals that reduce real risk; move others to L1 for review.</li><li><strong>No customer named.</strong> → If you can’t name the customer, you’re mapping the wrong flow.</li></ul><h3><strong>Suggested tools (optional)</strong></h3><ul><li>Mapping/whiteboard: Miro, Lucidchart, Whimsical, diagrams.net</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>Start each stage title with a <strong>verb</strong>; nouns create vague boxes.</li><li>If a stage takes &lt; 5 minutes consistently, it probably belongs with a neighbor at L0.</li><li>Put a <strong>⚠</strong> on any handoff that historically causes delay—these are your first WT targets.</li><li>Print the L0 and hang it. If the team says “that’s not how it works,” fix it <strong>now</strong> before L1.</li><li>Don’t automate anything until the <strong>L0 spine is stable</strong> for two weeks.</li></ul>$C$, 5, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 5 — L1 Stages + Swimlanes (Ownership)$S$, $S$lesson-5-l1-stages-swimlanes-ownership$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>At L1 you turn the L0 spine into <strong>6–12 executable stages</strong> with <strong>clear ownership</strong> and <strong>clean handoffs</strong>. Each stage lives in a <strong>swimlane</strong> (role/team), has <strong>one Accountable owner</strong>, explicit <strong>decision criteria</strong>, and a short <strong>acceptance checklist</strong> that prevents defects from moving downstream.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li>Converts big-picture flow into <strong>run-able work</strong> with zero role fog.</li><li>Exposes the handoffs where <strong>wait time (WT)</strong> and <strong>rework</strong> usually spike.</li><li>Sets the foundation for L2 procedures, per-stage metrics, SLAs, and later automation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end, you’ll have:</p><ol><li>An L1 map with <strong>6–12 verb-named stages</strong>, left-to-right.</li><li><strong>Swimlanes by role/team</strong> (not individuals).</li><li><strong>RACIQ</strong> for each stage with <strong>one</strong> Accountable owner.</li><li>A <strong>Complete-Input</strong> checklist at risky handoffs.</li><li>Decision diamonds with <strong>yes/no criteria</strong> (no vague branches).</li><li>Placeholder boxes for <strong>CT, WT, FPY, WIP</strong> per stage (to backfill from Lesson 3).</li></ol><h3><strong>Step-by-step</strong></h3><h4><strong>1) List your roles → create swimlanes (10 min)</strong></h4><ul><li>Use roles/teams only (e.g., Sales Ops, Implementation, Finance), <strong>not</strong> people’s names.</li><li>Put the <strong>end-to-end Accountable</strong> owner in the header (one human).</li></ul><h4><strong>2) Break L0 into 6–12 L1 stages (20 min)</strong></h4><ul><li>Each stage starts with a <strong>verb</strong> (e.g., <em>Capture Request → Validate Inputs → Prepare Work → Execute → Verify → Deliver → Bill → Close</em>).</li><li>If you need more than 12, you’re dipping into L2—pull back.</li></ul><h4><strong>3) Drop stages into the lane of the Responsible doer (10 min)</strong></h4><ul><li>The person/team doing most of the work owns the box’s lane.</li><li>Handoffs = line crossing into a new lane—mark them; these are WT risks.</li></ul><h4><strong>4) Assign RACIQ per stage (15 min)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>R</strong>esponsible (does)</li><li><strong>A</strong>ccountable (owns outcome) — <strong>exactly one</strong></li><li><strong>C</strong>onsulted, <strong>I</strong>nformed</li><li><strong>Q</strong>uality gate approver (only where risk justifies)<br /> Write names/titles next to each stage so there’s no ambiguity.</li></ul><h4><strong>5) Define Complete-Input checklists at handoffs (15 min)</strong></h4><ul><li>3–6 bullets that <strong>must</strong> be true before the next lane touches it (e.g., required fields present, files named to standard, approvals attached).</li><li>Work <strong>does not start</strong> in the next stage unless inputs are complete.</li></ul><h4><strong>6) Add decisions with explicit criteria (10 min)</strong></h4><ul><li>Diamonds are <strong>yes/no only</strong> (e.g., “Docs complete?”).</li><li>Label both exits; never leave a decision without criteria.</li></ul><h4><strong>7) Place per-stage metric placeholders (5 min)</strong></h4><p>Under each stage, reserve fields: <strong>CT (median), WT (→ next), FPY %, WIP</strong>. You’ll pull numbers from your Lesson 3 log.</p><h4><strong>8) Sanity pass &amp; tabletop (15 min)</strong></h4><ul><li>Walk one real case through the map with the owners.</li><li>Fix vague verbs, missing gates, multi-owner stages, and any decision without criteria.</li></ul><h3><strong>Quality checks (pass before moving on)</strong></h3><ul><li>6–12 stages, each <strong>verb-named</strong>, left→right.</li><li>Swimlanes show <strong>roles/teams</strong>; no individual names in lanes.</li><li>Each stage lists <strong>exactly one Accountable</strong> owner (RACIQ complete).</li><li>Handoffs include a <strong>Complete-Input</strong> checklist.</li><li>All decisions are <strong>binary</strong> (yes/no) with criteria.</li><li>Metric boxes exist beneath every stage.</li></ul><h3><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Noun boxes</strong> (“Intake Form”) → rename to <strong>verb</strong> (“Capture Intake”).</li><li><strong>Two Accountables</strong> → pick <strong>one</strong> or split the stage.</li><li><strong>Starting work with partial inputs</strong> → enforce the gate; bounce back incomplete work.</li><li><strong>Decision spaghetti</strong> → if you need 3+ branches, you’re in L2.</li><li><strong>People in lanes</strong> → keep lanes as roles/teams; people change, roles persist.</li><li><strong>Over-gating</strong> → approvals only where they <strong>reduce material risk</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Suggested tools (optional)</strong></h3><ul><li>Mapping/whiteboard: Miro, Lucidchart, Whimsical, diagrams.net</li><li>RACI tables &amp; checklists: Google Sheets, Airtable</li><li>Task boards (for WIP snapshots): Trello, Asana, ClickUp</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep stages <strong>time-meaningful</strong> (≥ 15 minutes of actual work on average). Tiny steps belong in L2.</li><li>Color the <strong>current bottleneck</strong> stage and the <strong>riskiest handoff</strong> (visual focus).</li><li>If a stage regularly needs help from another lane, create a <strong>C</strong>onsulted tag—not co-ownership.</li><li>Add a small <strong>SLA note</strong> under any stage tied to a customer promise.</li><li>Pre-name your files/records with a convention (YYYYMMDD_Object_DocType_v#) and put it in the <strong>Complete-Input</strong> checklist.</li><li>If owners argue about lanes, you’ve discovered a governance gap—<strong>resolve it now</strong> before L2.</li></ul><div class="lesson-template"><h3>📄 Template for this lesson</h3><p>Download and use the worksheet that goes with this lesson:</p><a class="dl" href="/templates/operations-workflow/raciq-ownership-grid.docx" download="RACIQ Ownership Grid.docx">⬇ Download RACIQ Ownership Grid</a></div>$C$, 6, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 6 — L2 Procedures (Quality by Design)$S$, $S$lesson-6-l2-procedures-quality-by-design$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>L2 turns each L1 stage into a <strong>repeatable play</strong> that any trained teammate can run. You’ll define the <strong>owner, required inputs, exact actions, acceptance criteria, outputs, and controls</strong> so work advances <strong>first-pass</strong> without ping-pong or surprises. Keep it tight, testable, and versioned.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Consistency at scale:</strong> New people can deliver the same result as veterans.</li><li><strong>Higher FPY, lower rework:</strong> Clear inputs and acceptance criteria prevent defects from traveling.</li><li><strong>Automation-ready:</strong> Deterministic steps + clean data make safe targets for bots/agents later.</li><li><strong>Auditability:</strong> Roles, timestamps, and gates are explicit—no guesswork in reviews.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end, each L1 stage has an L2 procedure with:</p><ol><li><strong>Owner (Accountable) + Responsible role</strong></li><li><strong>Required Inputs</strong> (complete-input checklist)</li><li><strong>Tools/Systems</strong> used</li><li><strong>Actions</strong> (ordered, concise, imperative)</li><li><strong>Acceptance Criteria</strong> (objective, 3–6 bullets)</li><li><strong>Outputs</strong> (data/files/state)</li><li><strong>Controls &amp; Exceptions</strong> (quality gates, edge-case handling, escalation path)</li><li><strong>Metrics &amp; SLA notes</strong> (CT, FPY target; any customer-facing promise)</li><li><strong>Version header</strong> (Owner, Version, Date, Next Review, Change Log link)</li></ol><h3><strong>Step-by-step</strong></h3><h4><strong>1) Create the procedure header (5 min)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Name:</strong> StageName — L2 Procedure</li><li><strong>Owner (A):</strong> Title + person</li><li><strong>Responsible (R):</strong> Role/team doing the work</li><li><strong>Version / Date / Next Review / Change Log link</strong> (store in your doc hub: Notion / Confluence / Google Docs)</li></ul><h4><strong>2) Define Required Inputs (10 min)</strong></h4><p>Write a <strong>Complete-Input</strong> checklist—work <strong>does not start</strong> unless every box is true.<br /> Examples (generic):</p><ul><li>Request/record exists in <strong>system of record</strong> (ID present)</li><li>Mandatory fields populated (owner, due date, priority)</li><li>Attachments named to standard (YYYYMMDD_Object_DocType_v#)</li><li>Approval or PO present (if required)</li></ul><p>Forms collection tools if needed: Google Forms, Typeform, Jotform</p><h4><strong>3) List Tools/Systems (5 min)</strong></h4><p>Declare the <strong>system of record</strong> and any companions. Example:</p><ul><li><strong>SoR:</strong> CRM/PM board (record authority)</li><li><strong>Companions:</strong> File store, e-signature (DocuSign), comms (Slack / email), password vault (1Password)</li></ul><h4><strong>4) Write the Actions (15–25 min)</strong></h4><ul><li>Use <strong>numbered imperatives</strong> (“Open record… Validate field X… Attach file…”).</li><li>Keep each step <strong>one action</strong>; multi-action lines create ambiguity.</li><li>Where choices exist, add the <strong>rule</strong> (“If criteria A, do X; else do Y”).</li><li>Cross-link any screenshots or mini-guides (recorded via Loom or built with Scribe).</li></ul><h4><strong>5) Set Acceptance Criteria (10 min)</strong></h4><p>Objective, testable, <strong>3–6 bullets</strong>. Examples (generic):</p><ul><li>All mandatory fields valid; no placeholders (“TBD,” “N/A”)</li><li>Required attachments present and named to standard</li><li>Status/state moved to {NextStage}; owner set; due date inside SLA</li><li>Customer/next team notified via system event (not manual DM)</li></ul><h4><strong>6) Define Outputs (5 min)</strong></h4><p>What <strong>state or artifact</strong> proves success? (e.g., “Record state = Prepared; checklist hash stored; confirmation sent; invoice scheduled”).</p><h4><strong>7) Add Controls &amp; Exceptions (10–15 min)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Quality gate:</strong> Who approves and <strong>what</strong> they check (RACIQ “Q”)</li><li><strong>Exception handling:</strong> Common branches (“If missing X, bounce to {Prior Stage} with reason code MI”)</li><li><strong>Escalation path:</strong> “If blocked &gt; 1 biz day, auto-escalate to {Role}”</li><li><strong>Rollback:</strong> How to safely undo if a downstream check fails</li></ul><h4><strong>8) Metrics &amp; SLA notes (5 min)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>CT target</strong> (median, from Lesson 3 baseline)</li><li><strong>FPY target</strong> (e.g., ≥ 90%)</li><li><strong>SLA</strong> if customer-facing (“Complete within 2 biz days of Trigger”)</li><li><strong>Event hooks</strong> for later automation (e.g., “On state change to Prepared, emit event ‘stage.prepared’”)</li></ul><h4><strong>9) Tabletop &amp; Dry-run (15 min)</strong></h4><p>Walk one real item through the procedure with the Responsible role, time it, and ensure <strong>every acceptance criterion</strong> is objectively testable.</p><h4><strong>10) Publish &amp; Train (20–30 min)</strong></h4><ul><li>Store in the shared hub (same folder structure across procedures).</li><li>Tag by <strong>Process &gt; Stage</strong>; link from the L1 map box.</li><li>Record a 3–5 minute walkthrough via Loom.</li><li>Add 5–10 <strong>check questions</strong>; track completion (LMS or simple form).</li></ul><h3><strong>Quality checks (pass before moving on)</strong></h3><ul><li>Inputs are <strong>complete and objective</strong> (no “use best judgment” to start work).</li><li>Actions are <strong>single-action lines</strong> with rules where paths split.</li><li>Acceptance criteria are <strong>binary</strong> (pass/fail) and cover the typical failure modes.</li><li>A specific <strong>Q owner</strong> is named where risk is material.</li><li>Exception handling &amp; escalation are <strong>written and routable</strong> (not “escalate as needed”).</li><li>Version header present; document stored in the <strong>single source of truth</strong> and linked from L1.</li></ul><h3><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Starting with fuzzy inputs</strong> → Enforce Complete-Input; bounce incomplete work with a reason code.</li><li><strong>Vague acceptance criteria</strong> → Replace adjectives with checks (e.g., “Valid phone = E.164 format”).</li><li><strong>Step salad</strong> (three actions in one line) → Split; 1 line = 1 action.</li><li><strong>Approvals that don’t add quality</strong> → Remove or move upstream; approvals should reduce real risk.</li><li><strong>Tool drift</strong> (people using different apps) → Declare <strong>SoR</strong> and integrate others <strong>to it</strong>, not around it.</li><li><strong>Orphan procedures</strong> (not linked) → Every L2 must be linked from its L1 stage box.</li></ul><h3><strong>Suggested tools (optional)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Doc hub &amp; knowledge base:</strong> Notion, Confluence, Google Docs</li><li><strong>Diagramming &amp; embeds:</strong> Miro, Lucidchart</li><li><strong>Screenshots/How-tos:</strong> Loom, Scribe</li><li><strong>Checklists / runbooks:</strong> Process Street, Trello, Asana</li><li><strong>E-signature &amp; forms:</strong> DocuSign, Typeform, Jotform</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep each L2 <strong>≤ 1 page</strong> of steps + 1 page of criteria/controls. Long procedures don’t get read.</li><li>Add <strong>examples</strong> next to criteria (1 good, 1 fail). It spikes FPY fast.</li><li>If a step fails often, add a <strong>pre-check</strong> to Inputs or a <strong>poka-yoke</strong> (required field, dropdown, template).</li><li>Embed a <strong>status change</strong> at the end of Actions so downstream teams see it <strong>without Slack pings</strong>.</li><li>Use the <strong>same field names</strong> in your docs and your systems to prevent translation errors.</li><li>Revisit each L2 <strong>monthly</strong> for the first quarter; then quarterly. Kill steps that add no quality.</li></ul><div class="lesson-template"><h3>📄 Template for this lesson</h3><p>Download and use the worksheet that goes with this lesson:</p><a class="dl" href="/templates/operations-workflow/l2-procedure-template-with-gate-acceptance-blocks.docx" download="L2 Procedure Template (with Gate & Acceptance Blocks).docx">⬇ Download L2 Procedure Template (with Gate & Acceptance Blocks)</a></div>$C$, 7, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 7 — L3 Task Steps (High-Risk/High-Variability Only)$S$, $S$lesson-7-l3-task-steps-high-risk-high-variability-only$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>L3 is the <strong>click-by-click</strong> layer you create <strong>only</strong> where errors are costly or variability is high. It turns an L2 procedure into a short, visual guide with exact fields, formats, and screenshots so new or rotating staff can hit <strong>first-pass</strong> quality.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li>Eliminates tribal knowledge at the most fragile steps.</li><li>Raises <strong>FPY</strong> and cuts rework where it hurts the most.</li><li>Creates safe ground for light automation later (deterministic rules, clean data).</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end, each high-risk L2 step has:</p><ol><li>A <strong>1–2 page</strong> L3 guide (max) with screenshots.</li><li>Exact <strong>field rules</strong> (format, allowed values, examples).</li><li><strong>Error checks/poka-yoke</strong> (what to verify before advancing).</li><li><strong>Rollback/exception</strong> notes and escalation owner.</li><li>Version header (Owner, Version, Date, Next Review) and a link from the L2.</li></ol><h3><strong>Step-by-step</strong></h3><ol><li><strong>Select only the right steps (10 min)</strong><br /> Use these triggers: high dollar/risk, frequent defects, complex UI, compliance exposure, or a step performed by many different people.</li><li><strong>Define the standard (10–15 min)</strong><ol><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> required docs/fields before you start.</li><li><strong>Output state:</strong> exact status that proves success.</li><li><strong>Quality bar:</strong> pass/fail conditions for this step.</li></ol></li><li><strong>Write the clicks (20–30 min)</strong><br /> Use numbered, imperative lines; one action per line. Example:<ol><li>Open the record in <strong>System of Record</strong> → <em>Search by RecordID</em>.</li><li>Set <strong>Status</strong> = Prepared.</li><li>Enter <strong>Due Date</strong> in YYYY-MM-DD format.</li><li>Attach file named YYYYMMDD_Client_DocType_v#.</li><li>Click <strong>Save</strong>; confirm green success banner.</li></ol></li><li><strong>Embed field rules and examples (10–15 min)</strong><ol><li><strong>Phone:</strong> E.164 format (+14145551212).</li><li><strong>Dates:</strong> YYYY-MM-DD (no slashes).</li><li><strong>Amounts:</strong> integers, no commas, cents as .00.</li></ol></li><li><strong>Add error-proofing &amp; checks (10 min)</strong><ol><li>Pre-check required fields; use dropdowns over free text.</li><li>Add a “Before advancing” mini-checklist (3–5 bullets).</li></ol></li><li><strong>Capture visuals (15–20 min)</strong><ol><li>Record a 2–3 min micro-walkthrough with <strong>Loom</strong> (<a href="https://www.loom.com">https://www.loom.com</a>).</li><li>Generate step screenshots with <strong>Scribe</strong> (<a href="https://scribehow.com">https://scribehow.com</a>) or annotate manually.</li></ol></li><li><strong>Exceptions &amp; rollback (10 min)</strong><ol><li>If validation fails, <strong>do X</strong> (link to fix).</li><li>If access denied, <strong>request Y</strong> (owner, SLA).</li><li>How to undo without corrupting data.</li></ol></li><li><strong>Publish &amp; link (10 min)</strong><ol><li>Store in your doc hub (e.g., <strong>Notion</strong> <a href="https://www.notion.so">https://www.notion.so</a>, <strong>Confluence</strong> <a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence">https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence</a>, or <strong>Google Docs</strong> <a href="https://docs.google.com">https://docs.google.com</a>).</li><li>Link the L3 from the exact L2 step; add Version/Date/Next Review.</li></ol></li><li><strong>Dry-run &amp; sign-off (10–15 min)</strong><br /> A fresh teammate runs the L3 end-to-end. If they stumble, fix wording or add a screenshot.</li></ol><h3><strong>Quality checks (pass before moving on)</strong></h3><ul><li>L3 exists <strong>only</strong> for high-risk/high-variability steps.</li><li>Max <strong>2 pages + micro-video</strong>; no walls of text.</li><li>Field rules are explicit and testable; examples included.</li><li>“Before advancing” checklist present; escalation/rollback defined.</li><li>Version header present; L3 is linked from L2 and lives in the single source of truth.</li></ul><h3><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Documenting everything at L3</strong> → Only the critical few.</li><li><strong>Vague field rules</strong> → Replace “enter phone” with format + example.</li><li><strong>Multiple actions per line</strong> → Split steps; one action each.</li><li><strong>Unlinked guides</strong> → Link L3 from the exact L2 step to keep it discoverable.</li><li><strong>Stale screenshots</strong> → Re-grab after UI changes; that’s what the Next Review date is for.</li></ul><h3><strong>Suggested tools (optional)</strong></h3><ul><li>Micro-videos: <strong>Loom</strong> (<a href="https://www.loom.com">https://www.loom.com</a>)</li><li>Auto-generated guides: <strong>Scribe</strong> (<a href="https://scribehow.com">https://scribehow.com</a>)</li><li>Screen capture/annotation: <strong>Snagit</strong> (<a href="https://www.techsmith.com/screen-capture.html">https://www.techsmith.com/screen-capture.html</a>)</li><li>Doc hubs: <strong>Notion</strong> (<a href="https://www.notion.so">https://www.notion.so</a>), <strong>Confluence</strong> (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence">https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence</a>), <strong>Google Docs</strong> (<a href="https://docs.google.com">https://docs.google.com</a>)</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>Write for <strong>scan-ability</strong>: numbers, bold field names, short lines.</li><li>Put the <strong>three most common failure examples</strong> right under the step they belong to.</li><li>End every L3 with a <strong>status change</strong> (so downstream teams see progress without Slack).</li><li>Add a quick <strong>3–5 question check</strong> at the bottom; it locks learning and gives you proof of training.</li><li>If a step gets changed <strong>weekly</strong>, fix the process first—then write L3.</li></ul>$C$, 8, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 8 — Instrumentation & Dashboard (CT, WT, FPY, WIP)$S$, $S$lesson-8-instrumentation-dashboard-ct-wt-fpy-wip$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>You’ll turn observed work into <strong>operational truth</strong>—clean timestamps, stage-level yields, and a simple dashboard that exposes the <strong>bottleneck</strong> and guides weekly decisions. We use <strong>medians</strong> for time metrics and track <strong>Aging WIP</strong> so nothing goes stale.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li>Replaces debates with <strong>numbers at the right granularity</strong> (per stage).</li><li>Surfaces where to act <strong>this week</strong> (constraint, aging items, SLA risks).</li><li>Makes automation/agent decisions <strong>evidence-backed</strong> and safer.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end you’ll have:</p><ol><li>A <strong>data schema</strong> for your event log (unit × stage).</li><li>Working calculations for <strong>CT, WT, FPY, WIP, Lead Time, Throughput, Aging WIP</strong>.</li><li>A <strong>one-page dashboard</strong> layout used in your weekly huddle.</li><li>Alert thresholds for <strong>bottleneck, aging, and SLA</strong> breaches.</li></ol><h3><strong>Step-by-step</strong></h3><h4><strong>1) Lock the event-log schema (from Lesson 3)</strong></h4><p>Minimum fields per <strong>unit × stage</strong>:</p><ul><li>unit_id</li><li>stage_name</li><li>entered_at (arrives in queue)</li><li>started_at (hands on)</li><li>finished_at (hands off)</li><li>owner (Responsible role)</li><li>first_pass (yes/no)</li><li>rework_count (0,1,2,…)</li><li>wait_reason_code (MI, AL, AD, TD, DQ, CP, SC, CD)</li></ul><p>Store in <strong>Google Sheets</strong> (<a href="https://sheets.google.com">https://sheets.google.com</a>) or <strong>Airtable</strong> (<a href="https://airtable.com">https://airtable.com</a>). If you need SQL + charts, consider <strong>Metabase</strong> (<a href="https://www.metabase.com">https://www.metabase.com</a>) or <strong>Power BI</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/power-platform/products/power-bi">https://www.microsoft.com/power-platform/products/power-bi</a>).</p><h4><strong>2) Compute the core metrics (weekly)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>CT_stage (median):</strong> finished_at − started_at (exclude idle).</li><li><strong>WT_A→B (median):</strong> started_at(B) − finished_at(A) for same unit_id.</li><li><strong>FPY_stage:</strong> count(first_pass==yes) / count(entered) over period.</li><li><strong>WIP_stage (snapshot):</strong> count of items started but not Done at a set time each day.</li><li><strong>Lead Time (median):</strong> Trigger → Done across units completed this week.</li><li><strong>Throughput:</strong> Units completed this week.</li><li><strong>Aging WIP:</strong> Oldest item age in each stage.</li></ul><p>Use <strong>medians</strong> for time; they’re robust to outliers.</p><h4><strong>3) Add stage data boxes to your L1 map</strong></h4><p>Under each stage show: <strong>CT (median), WT (→ next, median), FPY %, WIP (count)</strong>. Color the <strong>worst</strong> metric each week to direct focus.</p><h4><strong>4) Define thresholds &amp; alerts</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Bottleneck flag:</strong> Stage with highest <strong>effective time</strong> (CT + WT) or <strong>largest WIP</strong> for 2 consecutive weeks.</li><li><strong>Aging alert:</strong> Any item older than <strong>2× median CT</strong> for that stage.</li><li><strong>SLA risk:</strong> Items due within next <strong>24–48h</strong> that have not entered required stages.</li><li><strong>FPY watch:</strong> Stage FPY <strong>&lt; 90%</strong> triggers an acceptance-criteria review.</li></ul><p>Route alerts to your ops channel via <strong>Slack</strong> (<a href="https://slack.com">https://slack.com</a>) or <strong>Microsoft Teams</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams">https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams</a>). If you automate alerts, gate them behind a simple mute window to avoid noise.</p><h4><strong>5) Build the one-page dashboard</strong></h4><p><strong>Top row (global):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Lead Time (median)</strong> • <strong>Throughput (weekly)</strong> • <strong>Total WIP</strong> • <strong>Aging WIP (oldest item)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Middle (by stage):</strong></p><ul><li>A table: <em>Stage | CT (median) | WT→ (median) | FPY % | WIP | Oldest age | SLA risks</em></li><li>Heatmap cells (manual color or conditional formatting) to highlight worst offenders.</li></ul><p><strong>Bottom (action lane):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>This week’s constraint</strong> (1 line)</li><li><strong>Top 3 blockers</strong> (by reason code: MI, AL, etc.)</li><li><strong>Commitments</strong> (Who/What/By When)</li><li><strong>Next Review Date</strong> auto-filled</li></ul><p>Tools to build quickly: <strong>Looker Studio</strong> (<a href="https://lookerstudio.google.com">https://lookerstudio.google.com</a>), <strong>Metabase</strong> (<a href="https://www.metabase.com">https://www.metabase.com</a>), <strong>Airtable Interfaces</strong> (<a href="https://airtable.com">https://airtable.com</a>), or <strong>Notion</strong> databases (<a href="https://www.notion.so">https://www.notion.so</a>) with rollups.</p><h4><strong>6) Wire the weekly huddle (15–20 min)</strong></h4><p>Agenda (same every week):</p><ol><li><strong>Scoreboard:</strong> Lead Time, Throughput, Total WIP, Aging WIP.</li><li><strong>By Stage:</strong> CT, WT, FPY, WIP (skim for red cells).</li><li><strong>Constraint:</strong> Confirm the current bottleneck; is it unchanged?</li><li><strong>Blockers:</strong> Top reason codes (e.g., MI and AL spiking).</li><li><strong>Commitments:</strong> 2–3 actions, owners, due dates (update on dashboard).</li><li>Close with <strong>SLA risks</strong> and <strong>Aging WIP</strong> assignments.</li></ol><h4><strong>7) Keep data clean</strong></h4><ul><li>Validate timestamps (no negatives; finished_at ≥ started_at ≥ entered_at).</li><li>Standardize <strong>stage_name</strong> values (one canonical spelling).</li><li>Enforce reason-codes and first_pass entries (no blanks).</li></ul><h3><strong>Quality checks (pass before moving on)</strong></h3><ul><li>You can produce the five numbers <strong>on demand</strong>: Lead Time, Throughput, Total WIP, Aging WIP, and the <strong>bottleneck stage</strong>.</li><li>Every stage shows <strong>CT/WT/FPY/WIP</strong> from the last 20–30 items.</li><li>Thresholds and alerts are defined and routed.</li><li>Weekly huddle uses the <strong>same dashboard</strong> (no custom decks).</li></ul><h3><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Averages instead of medians</strong> → Outliers distort reality; switch to medians.</li><li><strong>End-to-end only</strong> → Stage-level metrics find the real constraint; add per-stage boxes.</li><li><strong>Metric pile-on</strong> → Keep to the essential seven; more metrics = less focus.</li><li><strong>No Aging WIP</strong> → Without it, quiet work <strong>expires</strong> in queues.</li><li><strong>Alert spam</strong> → Set mute windows and thresholds; alerts must trigger action, not anxiety.</li></ul><h3><strong>Suggested tools (optional)</strong></h3><ul><li>Event log &amp; quick calcs: <strong>Google Sheets</strong> (<a href="https://sheets.google.com">https://sheets.google.com</a>), <strong>Airtable</strong> (<a href="https://airtable.com">https://airtable.com</a>)</li><li>Dashboards: <strong>Looker Studio</strong> (<a href="https://lookerstudio.google.com">https://lookerstudio.google.com</a>), <strong>Metabase</strong> (<a href="https://www.metabase.com">https://www.metabase.com</a>), <strong>Power BI</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/power-platform/products/power-bi">https://www.microsoft.com/power-platform/products/power-bi</a>)</li><li>Alerts/comms: <strong>Slack</strong> (<a href="https://slack.com">https://slack.com</a>), <strong>Microsoft Teams</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams">https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams</a>)</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>Put <strong>Aging WIP</strong> at the top; it forces immediate triage.</li><li>If <strong>WT ≫ CT</strong> at a handoff, fix inputs or ownership <strong>before</strong> adding headcount.</li><li>Track <strong>FPY by stage owner</strong>—it drives better coaching than generic “quality” talk.</li><li>Use a <strong>WIP cap</strong> at the bottleneck before “working faster.” It stabilizes lead time.</li><li>Freeze the dashboard <strong>layout</strong>; changing the view each week kills pattern recognition.</li><li>Archive metrics <strong>monthly</strong> with a one-line narrative (“Contract redlines were the constraint; added templated clause—FPY +11%”).</li></ul><div class="lesson-template"><h3>📄 Template for this lesson</h3><p>Download and use the worksheet that goes with this lesson:</p><a class="dl" href="/templates/operations-workflow/metrics-calculator-dashboard.xlsx" download="Metrics_Calculator_Dashboard.xlsx">⬇ Download Metrics Calculator Dashboard</a></div>$C$, 9, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 9 — Flow Control: Bottlenecks, WIP Limits & SLAs$S$, $S$lesson-9-flow-control-bottlenecks-wip-limits-slas$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>Flow control is how you keep work moving at a <strong>predictable speed</strong>. You’ll identify the <strong>bottleneck</strong> (the step that governs throughput), cap <strong>WIP</strong> so queues don’t explode, and design <strong>SLAs</strong> that you can actually hit—based on your data, not wishes.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li>Predictable <strong>lead times</strong> → predictable <strong>cash</strong> and on-time delivery.</li><li>Lower <strong>rework and stress</strong> by stopping overloading.</li><li>Creates the operating surface for safe automation and staffing decisions.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end, you will:</p><ol><li>Name the <strong>current bottleneck</strong> using stage-level metrics.</li><li>Set <strong>initial WIP limits</strong> (system-wide and at the constraint).</li><li>Define and publish <strong>SLAs/SLEs</strong> grounded in your lead-time data.</li><li>Wire <strong>alerts</strong> for aging work and SLA risk.</li></ol><h3><strong>Step-by-step</strong></h3><h4><strong>1) Find the bottleneck (weekly)</strong></h4><p>Use your Lesson 8 numbers:</p><ul><li><strong>Effective Time per stage = CT(median) + WT→(median).</strong></li><li>The <strong>bottleneck</strong> is typically the stage with the <strong>largest Effective Time</strong> <strong>and</strong>/or the <strong>largest, growing WIP</strong> for ≥2 weeks.</li><li>Cross-check with <strong>FPY</strong> (low FPY at an upstream stage can fake a downstream bottleneck via rework).</li><li>Sanity test: If you gave this stage 20% more capacity, would the whole system go faster? If yes, that’s your constraint.</li></ul><h4><strong>2) Protect and elevate the bottleneck</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Complete-Input rule</strong> enforced <strong>before</strong> the constraint.</li><li>Put your <strong>best, least-interrupted</strong> people there; minimize context switching.</li><li>Pre-stage materials/data; automate <strong>only</strong> high-certainty prep steps.</li><li>If demand &gt; capacity for weeks, <strong>elevate</strong>: add skill, tools, or parallelize <strong>at the constraint</strong>, not elsewhere.</li></ul><h4><strong>3) Set WIP limits (system &amp; per stage)</strong></h4><p>Start simple and tighten:</p><ul><li><strong>System WIP cap:</strong><br /> WIP_cap ≈ Throughput_target × Target_Lead_Time (choose realistic targets from history).</li><li><strong>Stage WIP cap (including queue):</strong><br /> Start with people_at_stage × 1.5 (round down). Reduce until lead time stabilizes.</li><li>Post caps <strong>on the board</strong> (Kanban or status column). Enforce <strong>pull</strong>: new work <strong>only</strong> when WIP &lt; cap.</li></ul><h4><strong>4) Add an Expedite policy (rare use)</strong></h4><ul><li>One <strong>expedite lane</strong> with WIP = <strong>1</strong> system-wide.</li><li>Entry criteria must be <strong>objective</strong> (e.g., legal exposure, safety).</li><li>Every expedite displaces something else; track the cost and limit to <strong>≤5%</strong> of flow.</li></ul><h4><strong>5) Design SLAs you can hit</strong></h4><p>Use percentiles, not averages:</p><ul><li>From your completed items’ <strong>lead-time distribution</strong>, pick an SLE/SLA like <strong>“85% complete ≤ 7 business days.”</strong></li><li>Publish <strong>commit point</strong> (where the clock starts) and <strong>done</strong> definition.</li><li>Tie <strong>stage SLAs</strong> where customer-visible promises exist (e.g., “First response ≤ 4h”).</li><li>Review SLAs <strong>quarterly</strong>; adjust only after sustained capability changes.</li></ul><h4><strong>6) Wire signals &amp; governance</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Aging WIP alert:</strong> Item age &gt; <strong>2×</strong> stage CT(median).</li><li><strong>SLA risk alert:</strong> Items due in ≤48h that haven’t entered required stages.</li><li><strong>Reason-code trend:</strong> MI/AL/AD spikes mean inputs/approvals/access are the real problem.</li><li>In the <strong>weekly huddle</strong>, confirm the constraint and 2–3 actions due by next week.</li></ul><h3><strong>Quality checks (pass before moving on)</strong></h3><ul><li>You can state the <strong>current bottleneck</strong> and <strong>why</strong> (numbers, not vibes).</li><li>WIP caps are posted and enforced; people can’t start new work when the cap is hit.</li><li>An <strong>expedite policy</strong> exists (objective entry, WIP=1).</li><li>SLAs/SLEs are published with <strong>percentiles</strong>, <strong>commit point</strong>, and <strong>Done</strong> defined.</li><li>Alerts for <strong>Aging WIP</strong> and <strong>SLA risk</strong> route to a live channel.</li></ul><h3><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Raising WIP to “go faster.”</strong> → Lowers predictability and <strong>increases</strong> lead time. Cap it.</li><li><strong>Fixing non-constraints.</strong> → Improves local speed, not system throughput. Work the constraint first.</li><li><strong>Averages for SLAs.</strong> → Use <strong>P85/P90</strong>; customers feel tails, not means.</li><li><strong>Unlimited expedites.</strong> → They become the norm and nuke flow. Keep WIP=1 and ≤5% usage.</li><li><strong>Commit point drift.</strong> → If people start the clock early/late, your numbers lie. Lock the trigger.</li></ul><h3><strong>Suggested tools (optional)</strong></h3><ul><li>Kanban boards with WIP limits &amp; aging views: <strong>Kanbanize</strong> (<a href="https://kanbanize.com">https://kanbanize.com</a>), <strong>Trello</strong> (<a href="https://trello.com">https://trello.com</a>), <strong>Asana</strong> (<a href="https://asana.com">https://asana.com</a>), <strong>Jira</strong> (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira">https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira</a>)</li><li>Flow analytics / forecasting: <strong>ActionableAgile</strong> (<a href="https://actionableagile.com">https://actionableagile.com</a>), <strong>Nave</strong> (<a href="https://getnave.com">https://getnave.com</a>)</li><li>Alerting &amp; chat: <strong>Slack</strong> (<a href="https://slack.com">https://slack.com</a>), <strong>Microsoft Teams</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams">https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams</a>)</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>Put the <strong>bottleneck stage</strong> in a distinct color on your map and board; everyone should know it.</li><li>If <strong>WT ≫ CT</strong> into the constraint, fix <strong>input quality</strong> and <strong>handoff ownership</strong> before adding headcount.</li><li>Track <strong>% time the constraint is starved vs. blocked</strong>—both are flow killers with different fixes.</li><li>Set a <strong>daily WIP stand-down</strong>: before pulling new work, finish the oldest item in the constraint’s queue.</li><li>Publish <strong>SLE</strong> (internal expectation) and <strong>SLA</strong> (external promise) separately; don’t over-promise.</li><li>When SLAs slip, fix the <strong>system</strong> (WIP, inputs, approvals), not just individual effort.</li></ul><div class="lesson-template"><h3>📄 Template for this lesson</h3><p>Download and use the worksheet that goes with this lesson:</p><a class="dl" href="/templates/operations-workflow/wip-sla-policy-card-template.docx" download="WIP & SLA Policy Card Template.docx">⬇ Download WIP & SLA Policy Card Template</a></div>$C$, 10, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 10 — Ops Dashboard & Weekly Huddles$S$, $S$lesson-10-ops-dashboard-weekly-huddles$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>Your dashboard turns raw events into <strong>operational truth</strong> and your huddle turns that truth into <strong>action</strong>. One page, same metrics every week, same meeting rhythm. You’ll see the <strong>bottleneck</strong>, aging work, SLA risks, and top blockers at a glance—then commit to 2–3 fixes.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li>Ends opinion wars; decisions ride on <strong>the same numbers</strong>.</li><li>Keeps the team focused on <strong>flow</strong> (lead time, FPY, WIP) not busyness.</li><li>Creates a habit loop: <strong>measure → fix constraint → re-measure</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end, you’ll have:</p><ol><li>A <strong>single-page dashboard</strong> showing Lead Time, Throughput, WIP, Aging WIP, plus stage-level CT/WT/FPY/WIP.</li><li><strong>Thresholds &amp; alerts</strong> for bottleneck, aging, and SLA risk.</li><li>A <strong>15–20 minute huddle agenda</strong> with owner assignments and due dates.</li><li>A published <strong>meeting charter</strong> so the ritual survives leadership changes.</li></ol><h3><strong>Step-by-step</strong></h3><h4><strong>1) Lock the data feed (from Lessons 3 &amp; 8)</strong></h4><ul><li>Event log fields: unit_id, stage_name, entered_at, started_at, finished_at, owner, first_pass (y/n), rework_count, wait_reason_code.</li><li>Store in <strong>Google Sheets</strong> (<a href="https://sheets.google.com">https://sheets.google.com</a>) or <strong>Airtable</strong> (<a href="https://airtable.com">https://airtable.com</a>). For BI, use <strong>Looker Studio</strong> (<a href="https://lookerstudio.google.com">https://lookerstudio.google.com</a>), <strong>Metabase</strong> (<a href="https://www.metabase.com">https://www.metabase.com</a>), or <strong>Power BI</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/power-platform/products/power-bi">https://www.microsoft.com/power-platform/products/power-bi</a>).</li></ul><h4><strong>2) Compute the essentials (weekly refresh)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Lead Time (median)</strong>: Trigger → Done.</li><li><strong>Throughput</strong>: units completed this week.</li><li><strong>Total WIP</strong>: items started but not Done (snapshot).</li><li><strong>Aging WIP</strong>: oldest item age per stage.</li><li><strong>Per-stage</strong>: <strong>CT (median)</strong>, <strong>WT→ (median)</strong>, <strong>FPY %</strong>, <strong>WIP</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>3) Build the one-pager (freeze the layout)</strong></h4><p><strong>Top row (global):</strong> Lead Time (median) • Throughput (wk) • Total WIP • Oldest item age<br /> <strong>Middle (by stage):</strong> Table with columns → <em>Stage | CT (med) | WT→ (med) | FPY % | WIP | Oldest age | SLA risks</em></p><ul><li>Use conditional formatting/heatmap to flag worst cells.<br /> <strong>Bottom (action lane):</strong></li><li><strong>Current constraint</strong> (one line)</li><li><strong>Top 3 blockers</strong> (by reason code: MI, AL, AD, TD, DQ, CP, SC, CD)</li><li><strong>Commitments</strong> (Who/What/By-When)</li><li><strong>Next Review Date</strong></li></ul><p>Dashboards tools: <strong>Looker Studio</strong> (<a href="https://lookerstudio.google.com">https://lookerstudio.google.com</a>), <strong>Metabase</strong> (<a href="https://www.metabase.com">https://www.metabase.com</a>), <strong>Notion</strong> databases (<a href="https://www.notion.so">https://www.notion.so</a>) with rollups.</p><h4><strong>4) Define thresholds &amp; route alerts</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Bottleneck:</strong> stage with highest <strong>Effective Time = CT + WT</strong> or largest, rising <strong>WIP</strong> for 2 weeks.</li><li><strong>Aging alert:</strong> any item &gt; <strong>2× CT(median)</strong> for that stage.</li><li><strong>SLA risk:</strong> due ≤48h and not yet in required stage(s).</li><li>Deliver alerts to <strong>Slack</strong> (<a href="https://slack.com">https://slack.com</a>) or <strong>Microsoft Teams</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams">https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams</a>); include owner + link to the item.</li></ul><h4><strong>5) Publish the meeting charter</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Cadence:</strong> same day/time weekly, 15–20 minutes.</li><li><strong>Participants:</strong> owners of the constraint stage and adjacent stages, plus the end-to-end Accountable.</li><li><strong>Rules:</strong> no slide decks; dashboard only. Cameras on if remote. Timeboxed updates. Decisions logged.</li></ul><h4><strong>6) Run the weekly huddle (agenda)</strong></h4><ol><li><strong>Scoreboard (2 min):</strong> Lead Time, Throughput, Total WIP, Oldest item.</li><li><strong>By stage (6–8 min):</strong> skim CT/WT/FPY/WIP. Call out any red cells.</li><li><strong>Constraint (3–5 min):</strong> confirm bottleneck; choose <strong>one</strong> leverage move.</li><li><strong>Blockers (2–3 min):</strong> review top reason codes; assign owners.</li><li><strong>Commitments (2 min):</strong> record 2–3 actions with due dates.</li><li><strong>SLA/aging triage (2 min):</strong> assign owners for any items at risk.</li></ol><h4><strong>7) Close the loop</strong></h4><ul><li>Track <strong>commitment completion</strong> week to week.</li><li>If the constraint doesn’t move for 2–3 weeks, escalate: revisit WIP caps, inputs, capacity, or approvals.</li></ul><h3><strong>Quality checks (pass before moving on)</strong></h3><ul><li>One page shows <strong>global + stage metrics</strong>; no extra tabs in the huddle.</li><li>Thresholds/alerts are live and route to an owned channel.</li><li>Huddle occurs <strong>every week</strong> with the same agenda and the same board.</li><li>Actions have <strong>owners and dates</strong>; next week opens with status on last week’s actions.</li></ul><h3><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Shiny dashboards with 20 charts.</strong> → Pare to essentials; add detail only off-meeting.</li><li><strong>Averages instead of medians.</strong> → Switch to medians for CT/WT/Lead Time.</li><li><strong>Changing the layout weekly.</strong> → Freeze it; pattern recognition matters.</li><li><strong>No Aging WIP.</strong> → Add it; it forces real triage.</li><li><strong>Action sprawl (10+ tasks).</strong> → Cap to <strong>2–3</strong>; finish what you start.</li><li><strong>Ignoring reason codes.</strong> → They point to systemic fixes (inputs, access, approvals).</li></ul><h3><strong>Suggested tools (optional)</strong></h3><ul><li>Dashboards: <strong>Looker Studio</strong> (<a href="https://lookerstudio.google.com">https://lookerstudio.google.com</a>), <strong>Metabase</strong> (<a href="https://www.metabase.com">https://www.metabase.com</a>), <strong>Power BI</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/power-platform/products/power-bi">https://www.microsoft.com/power-platform/products/power-bi</a>)</li><li>Task/commit tracking: <strong>Asana</strong> (<a href="https://asana.com">https://asana.com</a>), <strong>ClickUp</strong> (<a href="https://clickup.com">https://clickup.com</a>), <strong>Trello</strong> (<a href="https://trello.com">https://trello.com</a>)</li><li>Alerts &amp; chat: <strong>Slack</strong> (<a href="https://slack.com">https://slack.com</a>), <strong>Microsoft Teams</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams">https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams</a>)</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>Put the <strong>Aging WIP</strong> tile top-left; it sets the room’s priorities fast.</li><li>Label the <strong>constraint stage</strong> on your board in a distinct color; everyone should know it.</li><li>Tie at least <strong>one commitment</strong> directly to the constraint every week.</li><li>Keep a one-line <strong>monthly narrative</strong> (“Constraint: Contract redlines. Fix: pre-approved clause bank. Result: FPY +11%, Lead Time −3d”).</li><li>Don’t let the huddle drift into status theater—<strong>numbers → decision → owner → date</strong>.</li></ul>$C$, 11, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 11 — Software Stack Discipline & Integration Mapping$S$, $S$lesson-11-software-stack-discipline-integration-mapping$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>Your software should <strong>serve the workflow</strong>, not the other way around. This lesson shows you how to declare a <strong>System of Record (SoR)</strong> for each business object, map integrations end-to-end, set <strong>data contracts</strong>, and retire duplicate tools—so data flows cleanly and your ops don’t depend on copy-paste.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li>Kills double entry and “which record is real?” drama.</li><li>Speeds up handoffs and cuts WT by making data available where it’s needed.</li><li>Lowers tool spend and incident risk by removing overlap.</li><li>Creates a stable base for automation and agentic workflows.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end, you will have:</p><ol><li>A <strong>SoR Matrix</strong> (object → single source).</li><li>An <strong>Integration Map</strong> (systems, directions, frequency, ownership).</li><li><strong>Data Contracts</strong> (field dictionary, formats, validation rules).</li><li>A <strong>Tool Rationalization Plan</strong> (keep/kill/sunset).</li><li>Runbooks for <strong>errors, retries, and rollback</strong>.</li></ol><h3><strong>Step-by-step</strong></h3><h4><strong>1) List business objects → pick the SoR (30 min)</strong></h4><p>Common objects: Customer, Contact, Lead, Opportunity, Order, Project, Ticket, Asset, Employee, Candidate, Placement, Invoice, Payment, Vendor, Contract.<br /> Create a 2-column table: <strong>Object | System of Record</strong>. Exactly <strong>one</strong> SoR per object.</p><p>Docs hub options: <strong>Notion</strong> (<a href="https://www.notion.so">https://www.notion.so</a>), <strong>Confluence</strong> (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence">https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence</a>), <strong>Google Docs</strong> (<a href="https://docs.google.com">https://docs.google.com</a>)</p><h4><strong>2) Build the CRUD &amp; Ownership Matrix (30–45 min)</strong></h4><p>For each object, define <strong>who creates/reads/updates/deletes</strong> and in <strong>which system</strong>. Add the <strong>Accountable owner</strong> (person) for data quality. This exposes shadow tools and unauthorized writes to the SoR.</p><h4><strong>3) Define the Data Contract (45–60 min)</strong></h4><p>Create a <strong>Field Dictionary</strong> per object:</p><ul><li>Canonical field name, data type, allowed values, required? default?</li><li>Format standards: <strong>ISO 8601 dates</strong>, <strong>E.164 phones</strong>, <strong>ISO 3166 country codes</strong>, currency ISO codes.</li><li>Validation rules (regex/lookup), <strong>owner</strong> of the field, <strong>authoritative source</strong>, and <strong>transforms</strong>.</li></ul><p>Store beside the SoR Matrix in your docs hub.</p><h4><strong>4) Draw the Integration Map (45–60 min)</strong></h4><p>One diagram from <strong>Trigger → Done</strong> showing:</p><ul><li>Systems (boxes) with SoR labeled.</li><li>Arrows with <strong>verb</strong> (Create/Update/Lookup), <strong>direction</strong>, <strong>frequency</strong> (webhook/event, scheduled, on-demand), <strong>transport</strong> (API/CSV/SFTP), and <strong>owner</strong>.</li><li>Note retries/backoff, <strong>idempotency keys</strong>, and <strong>dead-letter queue</strong> (for failures) if used.</li></ul><p>Diagramming: <strong>Miro</strong> (<a href="https://miro.com">https://miro.com</a>), <strong>Lucidchart</strong> (<a href="https://www.lucidchart.com">https://www.lucidchart.com</a>), <strong>Whimsical</strong> (<a href="https://whimsical.com">https://whimsical.com</a>), <strong>diagrams.net</strong> (<a href="https://www.diagrams.net">https://www.diagrams.net</a>)</p><h4><strong>5) Choose the integration pattern (15–30 min)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Event/Webhook (push):</strong> near-real-time, low WT.</li><li><strong>Scheduled pull:</strong> simple, acceptable for low-urgency syncs.</li><li><strong>Batch/ELT:</strong> for analytics or warehousing (nightly is fine).<br /> Pick the <strong>lightest</strong> pattern that meets the SLA.</li></ul><p>iPaaS/orchestration (use one, not three): <strong>Zapier</strong> (<a href="https://zapier.com">https://zapier.com</a>), <strong>Make</strong> (<a href="https://www.make.com">https://www.make.com</a>), <strong>Workato</strong> (<a href="https://www.workato.com">https://www.workato.com</a>), <strong>n8n</strong> (<a href="https://n8n.io">https://n8n.io</a>)<br /> ETL/ELT: <strong>Fivetran</strong> (<a href="https://www.fivetran.com">https://www.fivetran.com</a>), <strong>Airbyte</strong> (<a href="https://airbyte.com">https://airbyte.com</a>), <strong>Stitch</strong> (<a href="https://www.stitchdata.com">https://www.stitchdata.com</a>)<br /> Warehouses: <strong>BigQuery</strong> (<a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery">https://cloud.google.com/bigquery</a>), <strong>Snowflake</strong> (<a href="https://www.snowflake.com">https://www.snowflake.com</a>), <strong>Redshift</strong> (<a href="https://aws.amazon.com/redshift">https://aws.amazon.com/redshift</a>)<br /> Reverse ETL: <strong>Census</strong> (<a href="https://www.getcensus.com">https://www.getcensus.com</a>), <strong>Hightouch</strong> (<a href="https://hightouch.com">https://hightouch.com</a>)</p><h4><strong>6) Security, identity, and secrets (20–30 min)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Least privilege</strong> to the SoR; external tools write through APIs, not direct DB edits.</li><li>SSO/MFA: <strong>Okta</strong> (<a href="https://okta.com">https://okta.com</a>), <strong>Microsoft Entra ID</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/azure/active-directory">https://www.microsoft.com/azure/active-directory</a>), <strong>Google Workspace</strong> (<a href="https://workspace.google.com">https://workspace.google.com</a>)</li><li>Secrets: <strong>1Password</strong> (<a href="https://1password.com">https://1password.com</a>), <strong>HashiCorp Vault</strong> (<a href="https://www.vaultproject.io">https://www.vaultproject.io</a>)</li><li>Log <strong>PII access</strong>, encrypt at rest and in transit.</li></ul><h4><strong>7) Error handling &amp; observability (30 min)</strong></h4><ul><li>Retries with exponential backoff; <strong>idempotency keys</strong> to avoid dupes.</li><li><strong>Dead-letter queue</strong> for failed messages; review daily.</li><li>Alert on failure rate and time-to-recover: <strong>Datadog</strong> (<a href="https://www.datadoghq.com">https://www.datadoghq.com</a>), <strong>Sentry</strong> (<a href="https://sentry.io">https://sentry.io</a>), <strong>Grafana</strong> (<a href="https://grafana.com">https://grafana.com</a>)</li></ul><h4><strong>8) Rationalize the stack (45–60 min)</strong></h4><p>Score each tool on: <strong>usage (MAU)</strong>, <strong>overlap (feature %)</strong>, <strong>cost</strong>, <strong>integration fit</strong>, <strong>incident history</strong>.<br /> Decide <strong>Keep / Replace / Sunset</strong>. For sunsets, write a 3-step plan: <strong>freeze new use → migrate data → decommission</strong> by a date.</p><h4><strong>9) Governance &amp; cadence (30 min)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Quarterly stack audit:</strong> usage, cost, overlap, incidents.</li><li><strong>Change control:</strong> sandbox → pilot → release → rollback plan → update SoR/Contracts/Map versions.</li><li><strong>Owner of the map:</strong> one named person; next review date visible on page.</li></ul><h3><strong>Quality checks (pass before moving on)</strong></h3><ul><li>Exactly <strong>one SoR per object</strong>; CRUD/Ownership Matrix complete.</li><li>Integration Map shows direction, frequency, transport, and owner for each link.</li><li>Data Contracts (field dictionary + formats) exist for core objects.</li><li>Error handling is defined (retries, idempotency, DLQ) and alerting routes to a channel.</li><li>A written <strong>Keep/Replace/Sunset</strong> decision exists for any overlapping tools.</li></ul><h3><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Two SoRs for one object.</strong> → Pick one; use others as <strong>consumers</strong>.</li><li><strong>Unscoped write access.</strong> → Enforce API writes with least privilege; no direct DB edits.</li><li><strong>CSV heroics.</strong> → If you’re importing the same CSV weekly, that’s an <strong>integration</strong>—automate it.</li><li><strong>Event name chaos.</strong> → Version your events (v1.order.created) and publish payload schemas.</li><li><strong>Free-text fields.</strong> → Replace with dropdowns/IDs; free text guarantees DQ issues.</li><li><strong>“Temporary” tools that linger.</strong> → Add sunset dates; no tool without an exit plan.</li></ul><h3><strong>Suggested tools (optional)</strong></h3><ul><li>Docs &amp; catalogs: <strong>Notion</strong> (<a href="https://www.notion.so">https://www.notion.so</a>), <strong>Confluence</strong> (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence">https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence</a>)</li><li>Diagramming: <strong>Miro</strong> (<a href="https://miro.com">https://miro.com</a>), <strong>Lucidchart</strong> (<a href="https://www.lucidchart.com">https://www.lucidchart.com</a>), <strong>Whimsical</strong> (<a href="https://whimsical.com">https://whimsical.com</a>), <strong>diagrams.net</strong> (<a href="https://www.diagrams.net">https://www.diagrams.net</a>)</li><li>iPaaS/automation: <strong>Zapier</strong> (<a href="https://zapier.com">https://zapier.com</a>), <strong>Make</strong> (<a href="https://www.make.com">https://www.make.com</a>), <strong>Workato</strong> (<a href="https://www.workato.com">https://www.workato.com</a>), <strong>n8n</strong> (<a href="https://n8n.io">https://n8n.io</a>)</li><li>ETL/ELT &amp; warehouse: <strong>Fivetran</strong> (<a href="https://www.fivetran.com">https://www.fivetran.com</a>), <strong>Airbyte</strong> (<a href="https://airbyte.com">https://airbyte.com</a>), <strong>BigQuery</strong> (<a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery">https://cloud.google.com/bigquery</a>), <strong>Snowflake</strong> (<a href="https://www.snowflake.com">https://www.snowflake.com</a>)</li><li>Reverse ETL: <strong>Census</strong> (<a href="https://www.getcensus.com">https://www.getcensus.com</a>), <strong>Hightouch</strong> (<a href="https://hightouch.com">https://hightouch.com</a>)</li><li>Identity &amp; secrets: <strong>Okta</strong> (<a href="https://okta.com">https://okta.com</a>), <strong>Microsoft Entra ID</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/azure/active-directory">https://www.microsoft.com/azure/active-directory</a>), <strong>Google Workspace</strong> (<a href="https://workspace.google.com">https://workspace.google.com</a>), <strong>1Password</strong> (<a href="https://1password.com">https://1password.com</a>), <strong>HashiCorp Vault</strong> (<a href="https://www.vaultproject.io">https://www.vaultproject.io</a>)</li><li>Monitoring: <strong>Datadog</strong> (<a href="https://www.datadoghq.com">https://www.datadoghq.com</a>), <strong>Sentry</strong> (<a href="https://sentry.io">https://sentry.io</a>), <strong>Grafana</strong> (<a href="https://grafana.com">https://grafana.com</a>)</li><li>API &amp; webhook testing: <strong>Postman</strong> (<a href="https://www.postman.com">https://www.postman.com</a>), <strong>Webhook.site</strong> (<a href="https://webhook.site">https://webhook.site</a>)</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>Maintain a <strong>one-page SoR Matrix</strong> and link it from every L1 map—everyone should know where truth lives.</li><li>Use the <strong>3×3 rule</strong>: only integrate when a task happens <strong>≥3 times/week</strong> or takes <strong>≥3 minutes</strong> each time.</li><li><strong>No new tool</strong> without (a) SoR impact note, (b) integration plan, and (c) <strong>sunset plan</strong> if it overlaps another tool.</li><li>Version your <strong>events and APIs</strong> (v1 → v2) and keep a <strong>compatibility window</strong> before deprecating.</li><li>Enforce <strong>idempotency keys</strong> on create/update calls to prevent duplicates during retries.</li><li>Stage everything in a <strong>sandbox</strong>; promote to prod only after a <strong>pilot</strong> with rollback ready.</li><li>When WT spikes, check <strong>access/approvals/data quality</strong> first—tool changes rarely fix bad gates.</li></ul><div class="lesson-template"><h3>📄 Template for this lesson</h3><p>Download and use the worksheet that goes with this lesson:</p><a class="dl" href="/templates/operations-workflow/overview-instructions-for-template-8-system-of-record.docx" download="Overview & Instructions for Template 8 — System-of-Record +.docx">⬇ Download System-of-Record + Integration Map</a></div>$C$, 12, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 12 — Automation Opportunity Scoring & Readiness Gates$S$, $S$lesson-12-automation-opportunity-scoring-readiness-gates$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>Automation should remove <strong>redundancy, wait, and defects</strong>—not add chaos. This lesson shows you how to <strong>find</strong> the right candidates, <strong>score</strong> them, and <strong>green-light</strong> builds only after the workflow is stable and measurable.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li>Focuses limited build time on <strong>high-impact, low-risk</strong> wins.</li><li>Prevents “automating chaos,” which scales defects.</li><li>Gives you a repeatable gate so every automation is <strong>auditable and reversible</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end you will have:</p><ol><li>A ranked <strong>automation backlog</strong> using a simple scoring model.</li><li>Clear <strong>readiness gates</strong> every candidate must pass.</li><li>A <strong>pilot plan</strong> (10–20 cases) with success metrics and rollback.</li><li>A lightweight <strong>governance &amp; observability</strong> standard.</li></ol><h3><strong>Step-by-step</strong></h3><h4><strong>1) Surface candidates from your L1/L2 maps (15–30 min)</strong></h4><p>Look for steps that are:</p><ul><li><strong>High frequency + low variability</strong> (status updates, routing, renames, reconciliations).</li><li><strong>Deterministic decisions</strong> (“if X then Y”) with structured inputs/outputs.</li><li><strong>Wait-heavy handoffs</strong> caused by notifications or missing nudges.</li><li><strong>Rework-prone</strong> due to format/validation errors (pre-checks help).</li></ul><p>Use blockers from Lessons 3/8 reason codes (MI, AL, AD, TD, DQ, CP, SC, CD) to find pain that automation can relieve.</p><h4><strong>2) Score each candidate (1–5 scale per factor)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Volume:</strong> executions per week</li><li><strong>Minutes per execution:</strong> hands-on time saved</li><li><strong>Error impact:</strong> cost/risk of mistakes</li><li><strong>Rule clarity:</strong> 1 = judgment-heavy, 5 = deterministic</li><li><strong>Data quality:</strong> 1 = messy/free text, 5 = validated/standardized</li></ul><p><strong>Total score = sum of five factors</strong> (max 25). Prioritize the top items.</p><h4><strong>3) Do the quick ROI math (10 min each)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Monthly benefit ≈</strong> minutes_saved/run × runs/month × loaded_rate × expected_success_rate</li><li><strong>Monthly cost ≈</strong> licenses + (build_hours × builder_rate ÷ 12) + maintenance</li><li><strong>Net/month = benefit − cost</strong> (target positive in ≤2–3 months)</li></ul><h4><strong>4) Apply readiness gates (must pass all)</strong></h4><ol><li><strong>Process stability:</strong> L1/L2 versioned; no weekly changes.</li><li><strong>Owner &amp; acceptance criteria:</strong> named human; pass/fail defined.</li><li><strong>Complete inputs:</strong> required fields and formats enforced.</li><li><strong>Metrics baseline:</strong> CT/WT/FPY for the step exist.</li><li><strong>Exception/rollback plan:</strong> documented and routeable.</li><li><strong>Security:</strong> least-privilege access defined; secrets managed.</li><li><strong>Pilot scope:</strong> 10–20 cases; success ≥95% before scale.</li></ol><h4><strong>5) Design the pilot (fast)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Trigger:</strong> event/schedule/manual (be explicit).</li><li><strong>Actions:</strong> list exact calls/updates the bot will perform.</li><li><strong>Confidence test:</strong> define when to <strong>escalate to a human</strong>.</li><li><strong>Instrumentation:</strong> log inputs, outputs, decisions, duration, confidence.</li><li><strong>Rollback:</strong> single command to undo bot changes (or write-behind with approval queue).</li></ul><h4><strong>6) Choose the lightest fit tool (one, not three)</strong></h4><ul><li>iPaaS/automation: <strong>Zapier</strong> (<a href="https://zapier.com">https://zapier.com</a>), <strong>Make</strong> (<a href="https://www.make.com">https://www.make.com</a>), <strong>Workato</strong> (<a href="https://www.workato.com">https://www.workato.com</a>), <strong>n8n</strong> (<a href="https://n8n.io">https://n8n.io</a>)</li><li>Doc e-sign/creation: <strong>DocuSign</strong> (<a href="https://www.docusign.com">https://www.docusign.com</a>), <strong>PandaDoc</strong> (<a href="https://www.pandadoc.com">https://www.pandadoc.com</a>)</li><li>Data validation/transforms: <strong>Airtable</strong> (<a href="https://airtable.com">https://airtable.com</a>), <strong>Google Sheets</strong> (<a href="https://sheets.google.com">https://sheets.google.com</a>) + Apps Script (<a href="https://workspace.google.com/products/apps-script/">https://workspace.google.com/products/apps-script/</a>)</li><li>Webhooks/API tests: <strong>Postman</strong> (<a href="https://www.postman.com">https://www.postman.com</a>), <strong>Webhook.site</strong> (<a href="https://webhook.site">https://webhook.site</a>)</li></ul><h4><strong>7) Observability &amp; governance (minimum standard)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Logs:</strong> timestamp, actor (bot), input, output, result, confidence, duration.</li><li><strong>Alerts:</strong> failure rate, time-to-recover, cost/run to <strong>Slack</strong> (<a href="https://slack.com">https://slack.com</a>) or <strong>Microsoft Teams</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams">https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams</a>).</li><li><strong>Change control:</strong> sandbox → pilot → release; version your workflows; keep a <strong>changelog</strong>.</li><li><strong>Access &amp; secrets:</strong> store in <strong>1Password</strong> (<a href="https://1password.com">https://1password.com</a>) or <strong>HashiCorp Vault</strong> (<a href="https://www.vaultproject.io">https://www.vaultproject.io</a>); rotate quarterly.</li><li><strong>Ownership:</strong> a named human is responsible for performance and incident response.</li></ul><h3><strong>Quality checks (pass before moving on)</strong></h3><ul><li>Top candidates scored and ranked (documented).</li><li>Each selected candidate <strong>passes all readiness gates</strong>.</li><li>Pilot plan includes <strong>success metric</strong>, <strong>escalation</strong>, and <strong>rollback</strong>.</li><li>Logging and alerting are in place from day one.</li><li>A single owner is accountable for the automation’s outcome.</li></ul><h3><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Automating unstable steps.</strong> → Freeze the process first; two stable weeks minimum.</li><li><strong>Fuzzy rules.</strong> → Rewrite branches as explicit criteria; add validations upstream.</li><li><strong>No human-in-the-loop.</strong> → Escalate on low confidence or missing inputs.</li><li><strong>Tool sprawl.</strong> → Standardize on <strong>one</strong> iPaaS where possible.</li><li><strong>Silent failures.</strong> → Ship with logs/alerts; failures should page an owner.</li><li><strong>No rollback.</strong> → Always define how to undo bot-written state.</li></ul><h3><strong>Suggested example candidates (generic)</strong></h3><ul><li>Auto-create records from a form and set owner/SLA (Zapier: <a href="https://zapier.com">https://zapier.com</a>).</li><li>Validate fields (email/phone/date) and bounce incomplete inputs with a checklist (Airtable: <a href="https://airtable.com">https://airtable.com</a>).</li><li>Route items based on priority/tier and notify owner (Make: <a href="https://www.make.com">https://www.make.com</a>).</li><li>Rename and file uploaded documents to naming standards (n8n: <a href="https://n8n.io">https://n8n.io</a>).</li><li>Reconcile statuses between two systems of record nightly (Workato: <a href="https://www.workato.com">https://www.workato.com</a>).</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Automate prep, not judgment.</strong> Let humans decide; let bots gather, validate, and route.</li><li>Put a <strong>kill-switch</strong> (disable toggle) on every automation.</li><li>Start with <strong>one</strong> candidate per month; let the team feel the win.</li><li>Track <strong>time saved</strong> and <strong>FPY delta</strong> on your dashboard to prove value.</li><li>If <strong>WT ≫ CT</strong> and rules are clear, you’re staring at an automation win.</li><li>Document <strong>what the bot will not do</strong>—keeps scope tight and audits clean.</li></ul>$C$, 13, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 12.1 — Agentic Workflow Patterns (Human-in-the-Loop)$S$, $S$lesson-12-1-agentic-workflow-patterns-human-in-the-loop$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>Agentic workflows are <strong>event-driven helpers</strong> that can <strong>decide and act</strong> within a strictly defined scope. They <strong>don’t own outcomes</strong>—humans do. Use agents for triage, enrichment, routing, summarization, reconciliation, and monitoring once your process is <strong>mapped, measured, and stable</strong>.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li>Removes <strong>redundant clicks and waits</strong> without scaling defects.</li><li>Keeps humans on <strong>judgment and relationships</strong>, not data shuffling.</li><li>Creates measurable wins (time saved, FPY up) while preserving control and auditability.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end, you’ll have:</p><ol><li>A short list of <strong>agent patterns</strong> matched to your L1/L2 steps.</li><li>A <strong>guardrails spec</strong> (scope, permissions, confidence thresholds, escalation).</li><li>A <strong>build path</strong> (trigger → actions → observability) ready for a small pilot.</li><li>A <strong>measurement plan</strong> (success, intervention, cost/run, MTTR).</li></ol><h3><strong>Step-by-step</strong></h3><h4><strong>1) Pick the right jobs for agents (from your maps)</strong></h4><p>Good fits after stabilization:</p><ul><li><strong>Triage &amp; Routing:</strong> classify and assign based on rules.</li><li><strong>Enrichment:</strong> fetch/validate missing fields; normalize formats.</li><li><strong>Summarization:</strong> condense long inputs into structured notes.</li><li><strong>Reconciliation:</strong> sync statuses/records across systems.</li><li><strong>SLA Sentry:</strong> watch deadlines/aging WIP and notify owners.</li><li><strong>Drafting:</strong> create first-pass emails/docs from templates.</li></ul><p>Avoid: policy exceptions, high-risk approvals, or steps with shifting rules.</p><h4><strong>2) Define the guardrails (one-pager)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Objective &amp; scope:</strong> what the agent will/won’t do.</li><li><strong>Allowed tools &amp; data:</strong> systems and endpoints the agent can access (least privilege).</li><li><strong>Trigger:</strong> event/schedule/manual (be explicit).</li><li><strong>Confidence &amp; escalation:</strong> e.g., “if confidence &lt; 0.8 or required field missing → escalate to {Owner}.”</li><li><strong>Rate &amp; cost limits:</strong> calls/minute, daily spend caps.</li><li><strong>Audit &amp; privacy:</strong> log inputs/outputs; mask PII; never move PII outside the System of Record.</li></ul><h4><strong>3) Design the interaction</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> exact fields the agent receives (validated).</li><li><strong>Actions:</strong> deterministic steps (create/update/route/notify).</li><li><strong>Outputs:</strong> state change, structured summary, or queued draft for human review.</li><li><strong>Fallbacks:</strong> on error or low confidence → park in an <strong>Approval Inbox</strong> for the owner.</li></ul><h4><strong>4) Instrument from day one</strong></h4><p>Capture per run: timestamp • trigger • inputs hash • actions • confidence • duration • result (success/escalated/failed) • cost.<br /> Alert on failure rate and <strong>time to recover</strong> (MTTR) to your ops channel.</p><h4><strong>5) Pilot small (10–20 cases)</strong></h4><ul><li>Target <strong>≥95% success</strong> and <strong>≤10% human intervention</strong> before scale.</li><li>Compare <strong>time saved</strong>, <strong>FPY delta</strong>, and <strong>SLA hit %</strong> to your baseline.</li></ul><h3><strong>Quality checks (pass before scaling)</strong></h3><ul><li>Scope, triggers, permissions, and escalation are <strong>written</strong> and approved.</li><li>Inputs are <strong>validated/standardized</strong> (complete-input rule holds).</li><li>Logs &amp; alerts go to a <strong>live channel</strong> with an owner.</li><li>Pilot meets success thresholds; rollback plan is tested.</li></ul><h3><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Automating judgment.</strong> → Keep agents on prep, checks, routing, drafts. Humans decide.</li><li><strong>No guardrails.</strong> → Write the scope, allowed tools, cost caps, and escalation path first.</li><li><strong>Silent failures.</strong> → Ship with logs/alerts; no logs = no production.</li><li><strong>Tool sprawl.</strong> → One orchestrator; don’t mix three.</li><li><strong>Prompt drift.</strong> → Version instructions; review outcomes weekly; lock changes behind approvals.</li></ul><h3><strong>Suggested tools (optional, pick the lightest fit)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Orchestrators / iPaaS:</strong> Zapier (<a href="https://zapier.com">https://zapier.com</a>), Make (<a href="https://www.make.com">https://www.make.com</a>), Workato (<a href="https://www.workato.com">https://www.workato.com</a>), n8n (<a href="https://n8n.io">https://n8n.io</a>)</li><li><strong>Agent frameworks (dev-heavy):</strong> LangChain (<a href="https://www.langchain.com">https://www.langchain.com</a>), LlamaIndex (<a href="https://www.llamaindex.ai">https://www.llamaindex.ai</a>)</li><li><strong>Prompt/Run observability:</strong> Langfuse (<a href="https://langfuse.com">https://langfuse.com</a>), Helicone (<a href="https://www.helicone.ai">https://www.helicone.ai</a>)</li><li><strong>Guardrails:</strong> Guardrails AI (<a href="https://www.guardrailsai.com">https://www.guardrailsai.com</a>), NeMo Guardrails (<a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/nemo-guardrails">https://developer.nvidia.com/nemo-guardrails</a>)</li><li><strong>Doc e-sign/creation:</strong> DocuSign (<a href="https://www.docusign.com">https://www.docusign.com</a>), PandaDoc (<a href="https://www.pandadoc.com">https://www.pandadoc.com</a>)</li><li><strong>Alerts &amp; chat:</strong> Slack (<a href="https://slack.com">https://slack.com</a>), Microsoft Teams (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams">https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams</a>)</li></ul><h3><strong>Agent pattern library (plug-and-play)</strong></h3><p><strong>A) Triage &amp; Routing Agent</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Trigger:</strong> new request created.</li><li><strong>Action:</strong> classify priority/tier → set owner/SLA → post summary to channel.</li><li><strong>Escalate if:</strong> low confidence or missing inputs.</li></ul><p><strong>B) Enrichment &amp; Validation Agent</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Trigger:</strong> record enters “Validate” stage.</li><li><strong>Action:</strong> verify formats (email/phone/date), fill geo/taxonomy, rename files to standard, set reason codes if missing.</li><li><strong>Escalate if:</strong> any required field fails validation.</li></ul><p><strong>C) Summarization Agent</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Trigger:</strong> long intake/email uploaded.</li><li><strong>Action:</strong> produce 5-line structured summary + to-do list; attach to record.</li><li><strong>Escalate if:</strong> summary confidence below threshold.</li></ul><p><strong>D) Reconciliation Agent</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Trigger:</strong> nightly schedule.</li><li><strong>Action:</strong> compare SoR vs. secondary system; correct allowed fields; queue exceptions.</li><li><strong>Escalate if:</strong> conflict outside allowed field list.</li></ul><p><strong>E) SLA Sentry Agent</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Trigger:</strong> hourly.</li><li><strong>Action:</strong> find items approaching SLA, DM owners with item link and quick actions.</li><li><strong>Escalate if:</strong> item remains unacknowledged after N hours.</li></ul><p><strong>F) Drafting Agent</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Trigger:</strong> state changes to “Ready to notify.”</li><li><strong>Action:</strong> generate first-pass email/doc from template; park in Approval Inbox.</li><li><strong>Escalate if:</strong> not approved/edited within SLA.</li></ul><h3><strong>Measurement (add to your dashboard)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Success rate (% runs without intervention)</strong></li><li><strong>Intervention rate (% needing human touch)</strong></li><li><strong>Containment rate (% tasks finished by agent end-to-end)</strong></li><li><strong>Cost/run &amp; monthly cost</strong></li><li><strong>Latency (p50/p95)</strong></li><li><strong>MTTR on escalations</strong></li><li><strong>Drift alerts</strong> (success down week-over-week)</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>Start with <strong>one</strong> agent in the most <strong>WT-heavy handoff</strong>; prove value fast.</li><li>Put a <strong>big red kill-switch</strong> (disable toggle) on every agent.</li><li>Keep prompts/instructions <strong>in version control</strong> (change log, owner, next review).</li><li>Route agent decisions into your <strong>Reason Codes</strong> so fixes bubble up (MI, AL, AD, TD, DQ…).</li><li>Never let agents write to the SoR without <strong>idempotency and rollback</strong>.</li><li>Celebrate and <strong>bank</strong> time-savings in the dashboard; it funds the next build.</li></ul>$C$, 14, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 13 — Incident Response & Post-Mortems$S$, $S$lesson-13-incident-response-post-mortems$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>Incidents are <strong>unplanned interruptions or quality failures</strong> that threaten delivery, safety, revenue, or trust. This lesson gives you a <strong>repeatable play</strong> to detect, triage, contain, communicate, fix, verify, and learn—without blame or chaos. You’ll formalize severity levels, roles, comms cadences, and a blameless review that feeds back into your workflows.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Speed + calm:</strong> Clear roles and timelines cut MTTR and panic.</li><li><strong>Trust:</strong> Proactive, predictable updates reduce escalations and churn.</li><li><strong>Prevention:</strong> Root-cause fixes fold back into maps, SOPs, and gates so it doesn’t happen again.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end, you’ll have:</p><ol><li><strong>Severity matrix (P1–P4)</strong> with business impact, examples, and SLAs for acknowledge/update/contain/resolve.</li><li>A staffed <strong>on-call &amp; escalation path</strong> with an Incident Commander.</li><li>A one-page <strong>Incident Lifecycle</strong> runbook and <strong>Comms Matrix</strong>.</li><li>A <strong>post-incident review</strong> format that drives preventive changes into L1/L2/L3, data contracts, and WIP/SLAs.</li></ol><h3><strong>Core definitions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Incident:</strong> Unplanned interruption or degradation of service/process quality.</li><li><strong>P1–P4 severity:</strong> P1 = critical (safety/legal/major revenue), P4 = minor/no customer impact.</li><li><strong>MTTA:</strong> Mean time to acknowledge. <strong>MTTC:</strong> Mean time to contain. <strong>MTTR:</strong> Mean time to resolve/restore.</li></ul><h3><strong>Roles (name actual people, not committees)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Incident Commander (IC):</strong> Runs the response, assigns owners, decides status.</li><li><strong>Tech/Process Lead(s):</strong> Diagnose/fix in their lane.</li><li><strong>Comms Lead:</strong> Internal + customer updates; status page if used.</li><li><strong>Scribe:</strong> Timeline, decisions, actions, timestamps (for the post-mortem).</li><li><strong>Customer Liaison (optional):</strong> Single voice to key accounts.</li></ul><h3><strong>Severity matrix (example starter)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>P1 (Critical):</strong> Safety, legal exposure, customer-wide outage, cash stops.<ul><li><strong>Acknowledge:</strong> ≤ 10 min • <strong>Update cadence:</strong> 30 min • <strong>Contain:</strong> ≤ 2 h • <strong>Resolve:</strong> ASAP/clock continues.</li></ul></li><li><strong>P2 (High):</strong> Material impact to a segment or SLA breach likely.<ul><li><strong>Acknowledge:</strong> ≤ 30 min • <strong>Update:</strong> 60 min • <strong>Contain:</strong> ≤ 1 business day.</li></ul></li><li><strong>P3 (Moderate):</strong> Limited scope, workaround exists.<ul><li><strong>Acknowledge:</strong> same day • <strong>Update:</strong> daily • <strong>Resolve:</strong> within sprint.</li></ul></li><li><strong>P4 (Low):</strong> Minor defect/no external impact.<ul><li>Triage into backlog; no live incident needed.</li></ul></li></ul><p>Tune to your reality and publish where all can see.</p><h3><strong>Incident lifecycle (use this every time)</strong></h3><ol><li><strong>Detect</strong> — Alert from monitoring or report.</li><li><strong>Triage</strong> — Assign <strong>IC</strong>, set <strong>severity</strong>, open incident record (ticket).</li><li><strong>Contain</strong> — Stop the bleeding (WIP cap, rollback, feature flag off, manual workaround).</li><li><strong>Communicate</strong> — Internal war-room + stakeholder updates on cadence; status page if P1/P2.</li><li><strong>Diagnose</strong> — Gather facts/logs; form and test hypotheses.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong> — Implement change (small, reversible steps).</li><li><strong>Verify</strong> — Monitor KPIs; confirm back to green.</li><li><strong>Close</strong> — IC confirms outcomes met; schedule post-incident review.</li><li><strong>Learn</strong> — Blameless post-mortem; preventive tasks assigned with owners/dates.</li></ol><h3><strong>Comms matrix (publish this)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Where:</strong> War-room channel + bridge call (e.g., Slack: <a href="https://slack.com">https://slack.com</a>, Microsoft Teams: <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams">https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams</a>, Zoom: <a href="https://zoom.us">https://zoom.us</a>, Google Meet: <a href="https://meet.google.com">https://meet.google.com</a>)</li><li><strong>Who:</strong> IC, Tech Lead(s), Comms Lead, Scribe, Liaison.</li><li><strong>Cadence:</strong> P1 every 30 min; P2 hourly; P3 daily; P4 none.</li><li><strong>Status page (if external):</strong> Statuspage (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/statuspage">https://www.atlassian.com/software/statuspage</a>), Freshstatus (<a href="https://freshstatus.io">https://freshstatus.io</a>), Better Uptime (<a href="https://betteruptime.com">https://betteruptime.com</a>)</li></ul><h3><strong>On-call &amp; paging (for time-sensitive ops)</strong></h3><ul><li>Rotations with <strong>primary/secondary</strong> and clear hand-off rules.</li><li>Page tools: PagerDuty (<a href="https://www.pagerduty.com">https://www.pagerduty.com</a>), Opsgenie (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/opsgenie">https://www.atlassian.com/software/opsgenie</a>), incident.io (<a href="https://incident.io">https://incident.io</a>)</li><li><strong>Auto-escalate</strong> if not acknowledged within the severity SLA.</li></ul><h3><strong>Tooling (pick the lightest that fits)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Issue/incident tracker:</strong> Jira (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira">https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira</a>), Linear (<a href="https://linear.app">https://linear.app</a>), Asana (<a href="https://asana.com">https://asana.com</a>), Trello (<a href="https://trello.com">https://trello.com</a>)</li><li><strong>Monitoring/telemetry (as applicable):</strong> Datadog (<a href="https://www.datadoghq.com">https://www.datadoghq.com</a>), Sentry (<a href="https://sentry.io">https://sentry.io</a>), Grafana (<a href="https://grafana.com">https://grafana.com</a>), New Relic (<a href="https://newrelic.com">https://newrelic.com</a>)</li><li><strong>Docs/PM for reviews:</strong> Notion (<a href="https://www.notion.so">https://www.notion.so</a>), Confluence (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence">https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence</a>), Google Docs (<a href="https://docs.google.com">https://docs.google.com</a>)</li></ul><h3><strong>What “containment” looks like in ops (non-software examples)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Stop intake</strong> into the failing stage (temporary WIP cap = 0).</li><li><strong>Rollback</strong> to last good procedure/version; re-enable <strong>Complete-Input</strong> checks.</li><li><strong>Manual bypass</strong> while you fix (e.g., spreadsheet workaround).</li><li><strong>Guardrails</strong> (dual approval at a risky gate) until root cause is fixed.</li></ul><h3><strong>Post-incident review (blameless, time-boxed 30–45 min)</strong></h3><p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Understand <em>how</em> the system allowed the failure—not <em>who</em> to blame.</p><p>Include:</p><ul><li><strong>Summary:</strong> What happened/impact/severity/time window.</li><li><strong>Timeline:</strong> Facts with timestamps (from the Scribe).</li><li><strong>Customer impact:</strong> Who/what/how long.</li><li><strong>Contributing factors:</strong> Process, data, tooling, staffing, policy.</li><li><strong>Root causes:</strong> Use 5 Whys or fishbone; tag <strong>reason codes</strong> (MI, AL, AD, TD, DQ, CP, SC, CD).</li><li><strong>What worked/what failed</strong> in response.</li><li><strong>Permanent fixes:</strong> Changes to L1/L2/L3, acceptance criteria, data contracts, WIP/SLAs, automation/agent guardrails.</li><li><strong>Verification plan:</strong> How you’ll know it’s fixed (metrics/tests), by when.</li><li><strong>Owners &amp; dates:</strong> Each action has a single owner and due date.</li><li><strong>Next review date</strong> added to the doc header.</li></ul><p>Store reviews where everyone can find them; link from the incident ticket.</p><h3><strong>Quality checks (pass before moving on)</strong></h3><ul><li>Severity levels, SLAs, roles, and comms cadence are <strong>published</strong>.</li><li>Every incident opens a <strong>ticket</strong> with an IC, severity, timestamps, and a war-room channel/bridge.</li><li>Containment <strong>stops the bleeding</strong> (WIP cap, rollback, or bypass) within the severity window.</li><li>A post-incident review is scheduled for <strong>P1/P2</strong> within <strong>5 business days</strong> and results in <strong>assigned preventive actions</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Vibes over severity.</strong> → Apply the matrix; debate ends.</li><li><strong>No single commander.</strong> → Appoint an IC; too many cooks = delays.</li><li><strong>Silence or spam.</strong> → Cadenced updates (short, factual, useful).</li><li><strong>Big-bang fixes.</strong> → Prefer reversible, incremental changes.</li><li><strong>Blame game.</strong> → Focus on system conditions; individuals operate within systems.</li><li><strong>No follow-through.</strong> → Preventive actions go on the <strong>same dashboard</strong> you review weekly.</li></ul><h3><strong>Suggested starter SLAs (tune later)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>P1:</strong> MTTA ≤ 10 min • MTTC ≤ 2 h • Updates every 30 min</li><li><strong>P2:</strong> MTTA ≤ 30 min • MTTC ≤ 1 biz day • Updates hourly</li><li><strong>P3:</strong> MTTA same day • Review in sprint • Updates daily</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>Put an <strong>“Open Incidents” widget</strong> on your ops dashboard with severity, age, and IC.</li><li>Auto-create the war-room (channel + bridge) from the incident ticket to save minutes.</li><li>Keep <strong>ready-made comms templates</strong> for P1/P2 (internal + customer).</li><li>Track <strong>MTTA/MTTC/MTTR</strong> over time; big wins often come from faster <strong>acknowledge/contain</strong>, not just the fix.</li><li>“Containment first, clever second.” Stabilize the system before hunting elegant solutions.</li></ul>$C$, 15, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 14 — Change Management & Version Control$S$, $S$lesson-14-change-management-version-control$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>Change management ensures updates to workflows, SOPs, tools, and data contracts are <strong>safe, reversible, and understood</strong>. You’ll formalize how changes are proposed, assessed, piloted, released, rolled back, documented, and trained—so ops stay stable while you improve.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li>Prevents “surprise” changes that spike WT, drop FPY, or break integrations.</li><li>Keeps every map/SOP in sync with reality (audit-ready).</li><li>Reduces incident risk with <strong>pilot + rollback</strong> discipline.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets</strong></h3><p>By the end, you’ll have:</p><ol><li>A <strong>Change Taxonomy</strong> (Standard / Normal / Emergency) with approvals.</li><li>A one-page <strong>Change Request (CR)</strong> format + risk/impact scoring.</li><li>A <strong>Pilot → Release → Rollback</strong> checklist.</li><li>A <strong>Versioning scheme</strong> and <strong>Change Log</strong> linked from every doc.</li><li>A <strong>Release cadence</strong> (monthly train) and a lightweight CAB (Change Advisory Board).</li></ol><h2><strong>Step-by-step</strong></h2><h3><strong>1) Define the change types</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Standard (pre-approved):</strong> low-risk, routine (e.g., typo fixes, link updates).</li><li><strong>Normal:</strong> medium/high risk; needs review + scheduled release.</li><li><strong>Emergency:</strong> to stop active harm (ties to Lesson 13 severities).</li></ul><h3><strong>2) Create the Change Request (CR) form (one page)</strong></h3><p><strong>Fields:</strong> Title • Owner • Type • Affected items (L0/L1/L2/L3, SoR, integrations, SLAs) • Problem/Goal • Impact hypothesis on <strong>CT/WT/FPY/WIP</strong> • Risk (Low/Med/High) • Test plan • Training plan • Effective date • Rollback trigger &amp; steps • Approvals.<br /> Store CRs in <strong>Notion</strong> (<a href="https://www.notion.so">https://www.notion.so</a>), <strong>Confluence</strong> (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence">https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence</a>), or <strong>Google Docs</strong> (<a href="https://docs.google.com">https://docs.google.com</a>). Track statuses in <strong>Jira</strong> (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira">https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira</a>), <strong>Linear</strong> (<a href="https://linear.app">https://linear.app</a>), or <strong>Asana</strong> (<a href="https://asana.com">https://asana.com</a>).</p><h3><strong>3) Do the impact assessment</strong></h3><ul><li>Metrics: expected change to <strong>CT/WT/FPY/WIP</strong>.</li><li>People: roles trained, new <strong>RACIQ</strong> ownership?</li><li>Data: SoR fields, validation rules, data migration?</li><li>Integrations: API/event changes, idempotency, retries.</li><li>SLAs/Policies: commit point or “Done” definition change?</li></ul><h3><strong>4) Risk rate &amp; approvals</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Low:</strong> 1 approver (process owner).</li><li><strong>Medium:</strong> owner + cross-functional reviewer.</li><li><strong>High/Emergency:</strong> CAB approval (process owner, ops lead, data/integrations lead; 15-min review).</li></ul><h3><strong>5) Versioning &amp; document control</strong></h3><p>Adopt <strong>SemVer</strong> style for ops docs: <strong>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH</strong></p><ul><li><strong>MAJOR:</strong> behavior change or retraining required.</li><li><strong>MINOR:</strong> new step/criteria, no retraining.</li><li><strong>PATCH:</strong> wording/fix only.<br /> Header on every map/SOP: <strong>Owner • Version • Effective Date • Next Review • Change Log link</strong>.<br /> For files, enforce naming: YYYYMMDD_Process_Step_vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.</li></ul><h3><strong>6) Pilot → Release → Rollback</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pilot:</strong> small sample (10–20 cases), success criteria, owner, duration, start/stop, and <strong>rollback trigger</strong> (e.g., FPY drops &gt;5 pts).</li><li><strong>Release:</strong> announce, train, set <strong>cutover time</strong>, freeze window, confirm links updated.</li><li><strong>Rollback:</strong> explicit steps to revert doc, config, data, and comms; confirm metrics back to baseline.</li></ul><p>Orchestrate in <strong>Trello</strong> (<a href="https://trello.com">https://trello.com</a>) or <strong>ClickUp</strong> (<a href="https://clickup.com">https://clickup.com</a>). Approvals via <strong>DocuSign</strong> (<a href="https://www.docusign.com">https://www.docusign.com</a>) if needed.</p><h3><strong>7) Governance &amp; cadence</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>CAB (lightweight):</strong> 30-min weekly to approve Normal/High CRs and schedule the <strong>monthly release train</strong>.</li><li><strong>Freeze policy:</strong> no Normal changes 48h before major launches.</li><li><strong>Audit trail:</strong> CR, approvals, pilot results, release notes stored with the doc.</li></ul><h3><strong>8) Training &amp; verification</strong></h3><ul><li>Micro-video via <strong>Loom</strong> (<a href="https://www.loom.com">https://www.loom.com</a>); 5-question check recorded in <strong>TalentLMS</strong> (<a href="https://www.talentlms.com">https://www.talentlms.com</a>), <strong>Trainual</strong> (<a href="https://trainual.com">https://trainual.com</a>), or a <strong>Google Form</strong> (<a href="https://forms.google.com">https://forms.google.com</a>).</li><li>Post-release: compare pre/post <strong>CT/WT/FPY/WIP</strong>; keep if better, iterate if not.</li></ul><h2><strong>Quality checks (pass before closing a change)</strong></h2><ul><li>CR completed with impact hypothesis and <strong>rollback</strong>.</li><li>Pilot met success criteria (or rolled back cleanly).</li><li>Docs updated (headers, links), owners trained, effective date set.</li><li>Change Log updated; next review date stamped.</li></ul><h2><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Shipping without docs updated.</strong> → Policy: “No doc, no deploy.”</li><li><strong>Big-bang changes.</strong> → Split into smaller MINOR releases.</li><li><strong>No rollback.</strong> → Every Normal/High CR must have a tested rollback.</li><li><strong>Silent changes.</strong> → Publish release notes on a consistent channel (Slack: <a href="https://slack.com">https://slack.com</a> / Teams: <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams">https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams</a>).</li><li><strong>Link rot.</strong> → Quarterly link check; central index of all L0/L1/L2/L3.</li></ul><h2><strong>Suggested tools (optional)</strong></h2><ul><li>Docs/KB: <strong>Notion</strong> (<a href="https://www.notion.so">https://www.notion.so</a>), <strong>Confluence</strong> (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence">https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence</a>)</li><li>Work tracking: <strong>Jira</strong> (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira">https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira</a>), <strong>Linear</strong> (<a href="https://linear.app">https://linear.app</a>), <strong>Asana</strong> (<a href="https://asana.com">https://asana.com</a>)</li><li>Diagramming: <strong>Miro</strong> (<a href="https://miro.com">https://miro.com</a>), <strong>Lucidchart</strong> (<a href="https://www.lucidchart.com">https://www.lucidchart.com</a>)</li><li>Approvals/e-sign: <strong>DocuSign</strong> (<a href="https://www.docusign.com">https://www.docusign.com</a>)</li><li>Training/LMS: <strong>Loom</strong> (<a href="https://www.loom.com">https://www.loom.com</a>), <strong>TalentLMS</strong> (<a href="https://www.talentlms.com">https://www.talentlms.com</a>), <strong>Trainual</strong> (<a href="https://trainual.com">https://trainual.com</a>)</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep a <strong>public “What changed this month”</strong> page with 5 bullets max.</li><li>Tie every change to a <strong>metric goal</strong> (e.g., “WT A→B −30%”).</li><li>Pre-announce effective dates; give teams a <strong>sandbox</strong> to practice.</li><li>Use a <strong>release train</strong> (same day monthly) to reduce chaos; emergency only outside it.</li><li>Archive old versions—not delete—so audits and reversions are painless.</li></ul>$C$, 16, true),
    (mid, $S$Lesson 15 — Continuous Improvement Cadence$S$, $S$lesson-15-continuous-improvement-cadence$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Overview</strong></h3><p>Continuous improvement is a <strong>repeatable rhythm</strong>—not random projects. You’ll install a lightweight PDCA loop (Plan → Do → Check → Act) that hunts the <strong>current constraint</strong>, runs <strong>small, reversible experiments</strong>, measures results on the <strong>same dashboard</strong>, and locks in wins through change control.</p><h3><strong>Why this matters</strong></h3><ul><li>Sustains gains after the initial cleanup—no “slide back.”</li><li>Keeps the team focused on <strong>one constraint at a time</strong>, preventing thrash.</li><li>Converts ops ideas into <strong>measurable experiments</strong> with owners and dates.</li><li>Feeds improvements back into L1/L2/L3, SLAs, and the software stack—without chaos.</li></ul><h3><strong>Targets for this lesson</strong></h3><p>By the end, you’ll have:</p><ol><li>A <strong>monthly improvement cycle</strong> tied to your dashboard.</li><li>A <strong>constraint backlog</strong> with scored candidates.</li><li>A <strong>one-page experiment format</strong> with metrics, owner, and rollback.</li><li>A <strong>quarterly review</strong> that re-baselines and retires dead steps.</li></ol><h2><strong>The Cadence</strong></h2><h3><strong>Weekly (keep doing Lesson 10)</strong></h3><ul><li>Run the <strong>Ops Huddle</strong> on the one-page dashboard.</li><li>Confirm <strong>current constraint</strong>; track last week’s actions.</li><li>Triage <strong>Aging WIP</strong> and <strong>SLA risks</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Tools:</strong> Dashboard in <strong>Looker Studio</strong> (<a href="https://lookerstudio.google.com">https://lookerstudio.google.com</a>) / <strong>Metabase</strong> (<a href="https://www.metabase.com">https://www.metabase.com</a>) / <strong>Power BI</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/power-platform/products/power-bi">https://www.microsoft.com/power-platform/products/power-bi</a>). Comms via <strong>Slack</strong> (<a href="https://slack.com">https://slack.com</a>) / <strong>Microsoft Teams</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams">https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams</a>).</p><h3><strong>Monthly (Improvement Sprint)</strong></h3><p><strong>Goal:</strong> Ship <strong>one</strong> meaningful, reversible change targeting the constraint.</p><p><strong>Plan (60–90 min):</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Pick the constraint</strong> from stage CT+WT and WIP trends.</li><li>Pull 2–4 <strong>root causes</strong> (use reason codes MI/AL/AD/TD/DQ/CP/SC/CD).</li><li>Draft <strong>one-page experiment</strong> (see format below).</li><li>Agree on <strong>owner, start date, and rollback trigger</strong>.</li></ol><p><strong>Do (1–2 weeks):</strong></p><ul><li>Run the experiment on a <strong>small sample</strong> (10–20 cases) or a <strong>single lane/team</strong>.</li><li>Keep normal work flowing; don’t stall the system.</li></ul><p><strong>Check (30 min):</strong></p><ul><li>Compare <strong>CT/WT/FPY/WIP</strong> to the pre-experiment baseline.</li><li>Capture <strong>narrative</strong>: what actually happened vs. expected.</li></ul><p><strong>Act (30 min):</strong></p><ul><li>If metrics improved and risks are acceptable → <strong>promote</strong> via Lesson 14 change control.</li><li>If not → <strong>roll back</strong>, document lesson, and choose the next lever.</li></ul><p><strong>Work tracking:</strong> <strong>Asana</strong> (<a href="https://asana.com">https://asana.com</a>) / <strong>ClickUp</strong> (<a href="https://clickup.com">https://clickup.com</a>) / <strong>Trello</strong> (<a href="https://trello.com">https://trello.com</a>) / <strong>Jira</strong> (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira">https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira</a>). Docs hub: <strong>Notion</strong> (<a href="https://www.notion.so">https://www.notion.so</a>) / <strong>Confluence</strong> (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence">https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence</a>).</p><h3><strong>Quarterly (Re-baseline &amp; Retire)</strong></h3><ol><li><strong>Re-baseline metrics</strong> on a larger sample (last 60–90 days).</li><li><strong>Retire dead steps</strong> (merge, remove, or move upstream).</li><li><strong>Audit SLAs &amp; WIP caps</strong> against new capabilities.</li><li><strong>Stack audit</strong> (Lesson 11): usage, overlap, cost, incident history.</li><li>Refresh <strong>training</strong> and <strong>Next Review</strong> dates on all L1/L2/L3.</li></ol><h2><strong>One-Page Experiment (A3-lite)</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Title:</strong> Clear and short (“Reduce WT A→B by 30%”).</li><li><strong>Owner / Start / End / Rollback trigger</strong> (e.g., FPY −5 pts).</li><li><strong>Current state:</strong> Numbers + constraint stage.</li><li><strong>Hypothesis:</strong> “If we enforce Complete-Input + WIP=5 before {Stage}, lead time will drop.”</li><li><strong>Change:</strong> The <strong>one</strong> thing you will try (gate, WIP cap, pre-check, staffing tweak, tiny automation).</li><li><strong>Plan:</strong> Scope, sample size, and timeline.</li><li><strong>Metrics:</strong> CT, WT, FPY, WIP (stage), <strong>Lead Time</strong> (end-to-end).</li><li><strong>Risks &amp; mitigations:</strong> Access, approvals, customer impact.</li><li><strong>Result:</strong> Keep / Iterate / Roll back.<br /> Store in <strong>Google Docs</strong> (<a href="https://docs.google.com">https://docs.google.com</a>) or <strong>Notion</strong> (<a href="https://www.notion.so">https://www.notion.so</a>) and link from the dashboard.</li></ul><h2><strong>Building the Constraint Backlog</strong></h2><p>Score each idea (1–5) on:</p><ul><li><strong>Impact on constraint</strong> (how much CT/WT drops if it works)</li><li><strong>Effort</strong> (time/cost/coordination)</li><li><strong>Reversibility</strong> (ease of rollback)</li><li><strong>Time-to-learn</strong> (how fast results show)</li><li><strong>Risk</strong> (customer/compliance/integration)<br /> Pick the <strong>top 1</strong> each month. Everything else waits.</li></ul><h2><strong>What to Try First (generic levers)</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Before the constraint:</strong> Enforce <strong>Complete-Input</strong>, add pre-checks/validation, auto-assemble materials.</li><li><strong>At the constraint:</strong> <strong>WIP cap</strong>, best people, minimize interruptions, parallelize identical tasks, micro-automation for prep.</li><li><strong>After the constraint:</strong> Pull <strong>quality checks upstream</strong>, simplify approvals, automate notifications.</li><li><strong>Stack:</strong> Remove duplicate tools; declare SoR; add a single integration that eliminates copy-paste.</li></ul><h2><strong>Quality checks (pass before closing the month)</strong></h2><ul><li>One experiment <strong>started and finished</strong> (no carry-over without a decision).</li><li>Metrics compared to baseline; decision recorded (<strong>Keep/Iterate/Roll back</strong>).</li><li>If “Keep,” changes shipped via Lesson 14 (version, train, release notes).</li><li>Constraint backlog updated; next month’s candidate selected.</li></ul><h2><strong>Common mistakes (and fixes)</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Too many initiatives at once.</strong> → One change/month; finish what you start.</li><li><strong>No rollback plan.</strong> → Every experiment gets an explicit rollback trigger and steps.</li><li><strong>Big-bang experiments.</strong> → Shrink scope; learn fast on a small sample.</li><li><strong>New fires every week.</strong> → You’re ignoring WIP caps or gating—fix governance first.</li><li><strong>Changing the dashboard.</strong> → Freeze layout; only numbers change.</li><li><strong>Skipping training.</strong> → Promotion without training erases gains.</li></ul><h2><strong>Suggested tools (optional)</strong></h2><ul><li>Docs &amp; A3s: <strong>Notion</strong> (<a href="https://www.notion.so">https://www.notion.so</a>), <strong>Google Docs</strong> (<a href="https://docs.google.com">https://docs.google.com</a>)</li><li>Work tracking: <strong>Asana</strong> (<a href="https://asana.com">https://asana.com</a>), <strong>ClickUp</strong> (<a href="https://clickup.com">https://clickup.com</a>), <strong>Trello</strong> (<a href="https://trello.com">https://trello.com</a>), <strong>Jira</strong> (<a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira">https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira</a>)</li><li>Dashboards: <strong>Looker Studio</strong> (<a href="https://lookerstudio.google.com">https://lookerstudio.google.com</a>), <strong>Metabase</strong> (<a href="https://www.metabase.com">https://www.metabase.com</a>), <strong>Power BI</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/power-platform/products/power-bi">https://www.microsoft.com/power-platform/products/power-bi</a>)</li><li>Comms: <strong>Slack</strong> (<a href="https://slack.com">https://slack.com</a>), <strong>Microsoft Teams</strong> (<a href="https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams">https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-teams</a>)</li></ul><h3><strong>Hot Tips</strong></h3><ul><li>Put a <strong>“This Month’s Hypothesis”</strong> box on the dashboard so everyone sees the bet.</li><li>Celebrate <strong>deliberate rollbacks</strong>—they’re proof your guardrails work.</li><li>Bank wins in dollars/time: (time saved × runs × rate)—this funds the next improvement.</li><li>Track <strong>% of experiments that stick</strong>; if it’s &lt;50%, your ideas are too big or unscoped.</li><li>When in doubt, <strong>attack Wait Time before Cycle Time</strong>—WT reductions yield faster wins.</li><li>Tie improvements back to <strong>reason codes</strong>; if MI (missing inputs) tops the chart, fix inputs—not people.</li></ul>$C$, 17, true);
